# concepts.topic -- entry points to Botlish's language concepts: what each
# construct is, the restrictions that matter, why, and where to read more.

@topic concept.syntax
title: Lexical syntax: indentation, comments, names, literals, operators
summary: Botlish source is indentation-structured (spaces only) with `#` comments, names that may end in one `?`, and a small operator set with fixed precedence and no comparison chaining.
index: concepts
alias: lexical syntax; comment syntax; operator precedence; source syntax
keywords: indentation, spaces, tabs, comment, identifier, name, literal, operator, precedence, newline, brackets, trailing comma, escape
related: concept.bindings concept.functions concept.strings concept.integers rule.indentation rule.names rule.comparison-chains error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md ELIF.md

== WHAT IT IS
A Python-like surface language. Blocks are introduced by `:` and delimited
by indentation; every construct lowers to the compiler's semantic HIR and
adds no meaning of its own.

  * Indentation uses spaces only: a tab in indentation is a syntax error.
    Blank lines and comment lines do not count.
  * `#` starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
  * Names are letters, digits and `_` (not starting with a digit),
    optionally ending in exactly one `?`: the predicate idiom (`empty?`).
    A `-` is the operator, never part of a name.
  * Literals: decimal integers (arbitrary precision, no leading zeros),
    strings "..." (escapes \\ \" \n \r \t), characters 'a', `true`,
    `false`, `unit`, lists [a, b], structs {x: 1}.
  * A statement ends at the end of its line. Inside ( ) [ ] { } newlines
    are ignored, so a long expression is wrapped in parentheses; trailing
    commas are allowed in parameters, arguments and lists.

== RULES
Operators, from highest precedence to lowest:

    postfix   f(x)  x.field  x.f(a)   (chain left to right)
    unary     -x
    *
    + -
    == != < <= > >=                    (no chaining: a < b < c is an error)
    not
    and
    or

Arithmetic, `and` and `or` are left-associative. There is no `/`, `%`,
`+=`, `++`, ternary operator or line-continuation character.

== WHY
One way to lay out a block and no implicit statement separators keep
programs uniform; refusing `a < b < c` avoids a form that means different
things in different languages.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
# operators and precedence
x = 3
total = (1 +
    2 * x)
[total, -x * 2, x > 2 and not false]
@caption A wrapped expression in parentheses; unary minus binds tighter
@value [7, -6, true]
@end

@example reject
a = 1
a < 2 < 3
@caption Comparisons do not chain
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:7
@end

== MORE
The complete grammar is at the top of surface/parser.tcl; docs/README.md
section 17 describes the surface language.

@topic concept.values
title: Values and immutability
summary: Every Botlish value is immutable -- numbers, strings, lists, structs, functions -- except MutableVector and MutableArray, mutable values changed only through named places.
index: concepts
alias: immutable values; value semantics; immutable data
keywords: value, immutable, mutable, copy, equality, ==, hash, reference, identity
related: concept.bindings concept.lists concept.structs concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array principle.immutability rule.equality capability.assignment
docs: README.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md

== WHAT IT IS
The value kinds are Int, String, UnicodeChar, Bool, unit, List, struct
(named and anonymous), enum case, function (a closure), coroutine handle,
ImmutableSet, MutableVector and MutableArray (plus the byte and C ABI
values of the systems layer). Operations never modify their operands:
`std::list::append(xs, x)` returns a new List and leaves `xs` as it was.

== RULES
  * No assignment exists: a name is bound once per scope (concept.bindings).
  * `==` and `!=` compare values structurally (lists element by element,
    structs field by field); there is no reference identity to compare.
    Functions, MutableVectors and MutableArrays have no equality: `==` on
    them fails at run time (EQUALITY).
  * MutableVector[T] and MutableArray[T] are the two mutable values. They
    are still values, not references: `w = v` is a logical copy, and a
    mutation (`v.push(x)`) must name a place -- a local, a parameter or a
    field path from one -- and changes only that place's value.

== WHY
Immutable values make every binding mean one thing for its whole scope, so
the compiler's facts about a value (its type, its range, a proven
refinement) stay true wherever the name is used, and functions can be
analyzed, specialized and run on any backend without aliasing questions.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

xs = [1, 2]
ys = xs.append(3)
[xs, ys, xs == [1, 2]]
@caption append returns a new List; xs is unchanged
@value [[1, 2], [1, 2, 3], true]
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
w = v
w.push(3)
[v.length(), w.length()]
@caption A MutableVector is a value: w is a copy, not an alias
@value [2, 3]
@end

== MORE
docs/README.md section 1 (values); MUTABLE-VECTOR.md, MUTABLE-ARRAY.md.

@topic concept.bindings
title: Bindings: `x = e` binds a name once
summary: `x = e` is an immutable binding, not an assignment: a second binding of the same name in the same scope is DUPLICATE, while a nested scope may shadow the name without changing the outer binding.
index: concepts
alias: let binding; variable binding; shadowing; immutable binding
keywords: binding, variable, assign, rebind, shadow, scope, DUPLICATE, let, const
related: concept.values concept.definition-order concept.destructuring rule.immutable-bindings capability.assignment principle.immutability error.duplicate
docs: README.md STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md

== WHAT IT IS
`name = expression` evaluates the expression once and binds the name to
the value for the rest of the scope. The statement's own value is the
bound value. Scopes are the program or module top level, a function body,
each `if` branch, a loop body and a handler.

== RULES
  * A name is bound at most once per scope (DUPLICATE otherwise); a
    function's parameters count as bindings of its body.
  * A nested scope may bind the same name again (shadowing); the outer
    binding is unchanged after the nested scope ends.
  * A binding is visible from its definition on, never before
    (concept.definition-order).
  * There is no type annotation on a binding (`x: int = 1` is a syntax
    error): types are declared on parameters, results and fields.

== WHY
Bindings that never change are what make Botlish's static facts reliable:
a refinement proven for `s`, or a range proven for `i`, holds wherever `s`
or `i` is in scope. Instead of updating a variable, bind a new name, carry
an accumulator as a parameter, or use a collecting loop.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
x = 1
if x > 0:
    x = 2
x
@caption The branch shadows x in its own scope
@value 1
@end

@example reject
x = 1
x = x + 1
x
@caption No reassignment: the second binding is DUPLICATE
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE
@at 2:1
@end

== MORE
docs/README.md section 17 ("Syntax"); capability.assignment for the
alternatives to mutation.

@topic concept.definition-order
title: Definition order: visible from the definition on, no hoisting
summary: A binding or function is visible only after its definition in the same scope; nothing is hoisted, so callees are written before callers and a forward reference is UNBOUND.
index: concepts
alias: definition order; hoisting; declare before use; callee first; forward declaration
keywords: forward reference, order, hoist, UNBOUND, declared later, recursion, callee, caller
related: concept.bindings concept.mutual-recursion concept.functions rule.forward-references error.unbound principle.bind-before-use
docs: STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md MUTUAL-RECURSION.md

== WHAT IT IS
Name resolution walks the source once, in order. A binding comes into
existence where it is written; nothing later in a scope exists yet. This
holds at a program's or module's top level, in a function body, a branch,
a loop body and a handler alike.

== RULES
  * A reference to a binding defined later in the same scope is UNBOUND
    ("... is declared later; forward references are not allowed"), even
    in a function that is never called or a branch that never runs.
  * A named function may call itself: `fn f` binds `f` before its body.
  * A value is not visible in its own initializer.
  * A closure sees what exists where it is created; a later binding of the
    same name never changes what an earlier reference meant.
  * Declarations that bind no value -- struct, enum, type, error, trait,
    layout -- may be used before they are written.

== WHY
When later definitions could be seen early, the compiler had to repair
what earlier stages could not know; measured on a CSV parser, the
caller-first order produced about 35% more emitted functions and twice the
run-time guards. With strict order, what a reference means is fixed by
the text before it.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn double(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

fn quadruple(n: int) -> int:
    double(double(n))

quadruple(5)
@caption The callee is written first
@value 20
@end

@example reject
fn quadruple(n: int) -> int:
    double(double(n))

fn double(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

quadruple(5)
@caption A call of a function defined later
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 2:5
@end

== MORE
Two functions that call each other cannot both see the other: see
concept.mutual-recursion. STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md has the rule and
its measurements.

@topic concept.types
title: Types, inference and annotations
summary: Types are inferred; annotations on parameters, results and fields are contracts the compiler must prove at each use ("cannot be proven to satisfy"), while untyped parameters make a function generic.
index: concepts
alias: type annotation; type inference; static types; type system
keywords: int, str, bool, unit, UnicodeChar, List, any, annotation, declared type, inferred, proven, contract, TYPE
related: concept.integers concept.strings concept.lists concept.structs concept.enums concept.refinements concept.integer-domains concept.function-types concept.specialization proof.types rule.typed-parameters error.type
docs: README.md STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md MINIMAL-APPLIED-LIST-TYPES.md

== WHAT IT IS
The built-in types are `int`, `str`, `bool`, `unit`, `UnicodeChar`,
`List[T]` and `any` (nothing known), plus the collection types
(MutableVector[T], MutableArray[T], ImmutableSet[T]), function types
(Fn{...}, Coroutine{...}) and the types a program declares: structs,
enums, integer domains, refinements and traits.

Types are written on parameters (`n: int`), results (`-> List[str]`),
struct and error-payload fields, inside function types, and in layout and
`extern c` declarations. Bindings are never annotated: everything else is
inferred.

== RULES
  * A declared type is a contract proven statically. An argument, a
    returned value or a field value that the compiler cannot prove
    admissible is the TYPE error ("argument for parameter "n" cannot be
    proven to satisfy int"); no run-time cast or guard is inserted.
  * An untyped parameter accepts any value; the function is generic and
    analyzed again per call where its argument types are known
    (concept.specialization).
  * Operations of the runtime (operators, intrinsics) check their own
    operands when they run: `"a" + 1` fails at run time with the TYPE
    error unless an annotation lets the compiler reject it earlier.
  * No type is callable: `T(x)` is not a conversion; a conversion is an
    ordinary function whose declared result is the type.

== WHY
Types describe values; they never change what a program means. Declaring
a type is how a function states what it requires and guarantees, and the
compiler proves those statements instead of trusting them.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn label(n: int, unit_name: str) -> List[str]:
    if n == 1:
        return [unit_name]
    [unit_name, unit_name]

label(2, "kg")
@caption Declared parameter and result types
@value ["kg", "kg"]
@end

@example reject
fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

twice("ten")
@caption The argument cannot be proven to satisfy int
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:7
@end

== MORE
docs/README.md sections 13 and 14; STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md.

@topic concept.integers
title: Integers: arbitrary precision, no integer division
summary: Int is an arbitrary-precision integer with + - *, comparisons, mod (Euclidean) and bit operations; there is no integer division; std::int converts it to and from decimal text.
index: concepts
alias: integers; arbitrary precision integers; bigint; bit operations; Int type
keywords: int, integer, arithmetic, overflow, mod, remainder, bit_and, bit_or, bit_xor, shift_left, shift_right, ARITHMETIC, decimal
related: concept.integer-domains concept.floats concept.types capability.integer-division task.number-text stdlib.mod stdlib.bit_and stdlib.shift_right stdlib.std::int::to_string stdlib.std::int::from_string proof.ranges error.arithmetic
docs: README.md BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md LANGUAGE-LIBRARY-DEFICIENCIES.md

== WHAT IT IS
`int` values are mathematical integers: they never overflow or wrap, on
every backend (the native backend switches to a big representation when a
value leaves the machine word). Literals are decimal.

    + - *               int, int -> int
    < <= > >= == !=     comparisons
    mod(a, b)           Euclidean remainder: 0 <= r < abs(b)
    bit_and bit_or bit_xor shift_left shift_right   (root, no import)

== RULES
  * `mod(a, 0)` fails at run time with the ARITHMETIC error; it is not a
    declared error and cannot be handled, so guard the divisor.
  * There is no integer division: `/` divides Float64 values only, and
    there is no `%` and no integer division function
    (capability.integer-division). `mod` gives the remainder, shift_right
    divides by a power of two.
  * An Int never mixes with a Float64 (concept.floats):
    std::float64::from_int, std::float64::truncate (toward zero) and
    std::float64::round (to nearest, ties to even) convert explicitly.
  * An Int's text is std::int::to_string(n) (import std::int): its decimal
    digits, a "-" when negative; std::int::from_string(s) reads exactly
    such a text back, else the declared error InvalidIntegerText
    (task.number-text).
  * bit_and, bit_or and bit_xor are `nomethod`: their operands are
    symmetric, so `a.bit_and(b)` is rejected.

== WHY
Arbitrary precision is a core promise of the language: arithmetic results
are exact, so range proofs about Ints (concept.integer-domains) reason
about mathematics, not machine words.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
big = 9223372036854775807 * 9223372036854775807
[mod(-7, 3), mod(17, 5), bit_xor(12, 10), shift_left(1, 70), big > 0]
@caption Exact arithmetic beyond 64 bits
@value [2, 2, 6, 1180591620717411303424, true]
@end

@example reject
x = 7 / 2
x
@caption There is no integer division
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:5
@end

== MORE
BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md for the bit operations and byte domains.

@topic concept.floats
title: Float64: finite binary64 floating-point numbers
summary: Float64 is a finite IEEE 754 binary64 number: literals such as 1.5, + - * / and comparisons; a result that is not finite (NaN or an infinity) fails with the declared error NonFiniteFloat.
index: concepts
alias: float; floats; double; floating point; decimal number; real number; Float64; NonFiniteFloat; NaN; infinity; float division by zero
keywords: float, double, decimal, fraction, real, divide, NaN, infinity, finite, NonFiniteFloat, from_int, truncate, round
related: concept.integers stdlib./ stdlib.std::float64::from_int stdlib.std::float64::truncate stdlib.std::float64::round stdlib.module.std::float64 capability.integer-division error.known-error error.unhandled-error concept.errors concept.decimal-rounded
docs: FLOAT64-MILESTONE-1.md README.md

== WHAT IT IS
A Float64 is a finite IEEE 754 binary64 number. NaN and the infinities
are never values: every operation that would produce one fails instead,
with the declared error NonFiniteFloat. There is no negative zero either:
-0.0 and 0.0 are the one value 0.0.

    1.5  0.0  -3.25  10.0  6.02e23   literals: digits, a point, digits,
                                     an optional exponent
    + - * /             Float64, Float64 -> Float64 (may fail: NonFiniteFloat)
    -x                  negation
    < <= > >= == !=     comparisons
    std::float64::from_int(n)    Int -> nearest Float64 (import std::float64)
    std::float64::truncate(x)    Float64 -> Int, toward zero
    std::float64::round(x)       Float64 -> nearest Int, a half to the even one
    std::float64::min(a, b)  std::float64::max(a, b)  x.abs()  x.clamp(low, high)
                        bounds (stdlib/float64.bot); they never fail

== RULES
  * A literal with a decimal point is a Float64, except where a
    DecimalRounded type is expected (concept.decimal-rounded), where it is
    a DecimalRounded constant of its exact decimal; a literal without one
    is an Int. A binding never decides a literal's type.
  * An operator's operands are both Float64 or both Int: the compiler
    chooses the Float64 operation by the operands' static types, and an Int
    and a Float64 together are rejected (TYPE). Nothing converts implicitly;
    convert with std::float64::from_int, std::float64::truncate or
    std::float64::round. round and truncate read the exact binary value and
    never fail: every Float64 has an Int nearest it and an integer part.
  * `/` divides Float64 values only (capability.integer-division).
  * A result that is not finite -- an overflow, any division by zero --
    fails with NonFiniteFloat, an ordinary declared error: a function
    whose + - * / the compiler cannot prove finite handles it or declares
    `errors NonFiniteFloat` (concept.errors). When the compiler knows the
    operands it computes the operation while compiling, with the same
    rule: a decided failure is the KNOWN-ERROR diagnostic. Negation and
    the comparisons never fail.
  * NonFiniteFloat's payload is {result: NonFiniteResult}, which
    non-finite result the operation had: PositiveInfinity,
    NegativeInfinity or NotANumber (only 0.0 / 0.0). NonFiniteResult is a
    builtin enum, visible everywhere without an import. It describes the
    failure to the handler where it happened; no value is ever an
    infinity or NaN.
  * 1.0 == 1 is false: values of different kinds are never equal.
  * A Float64 prints as the shortest decimal that reads back to the same
    number, always with a point (3.0, 0.1, 1.0e100).
  * C's float and double are C representations of Float64 values,
    std::ffi::c::Float (binary32) and std::ffi::c::Double (binary64): a
    Float64 a C float receives is rounded to nearest, ties to even, and one
    too large for it fails with NonFiniteFloat before C runs; a NaN or an
    infinity coming back from C, or read from a layout's bytes, fails with
    NonFiniteFloat (concept.c-floats).

== WHY
Finite values keep == an equivalence (no NaN that differs from itself)
and make every failure a declared, typed completion the compiler tracks,
instead of a special value that spreads silently through later
arithmetic.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn average(a: Float64, b: Float64) -> Float64 errors NonFiniteFloat:
    (a + b) / 2.0

x = 1.5
mean = x.average(2.5):
    on NonFiniteFloat:
        0.0
[x * 4.0, -x, x < 2.0, 1.0e100 * 1.0e100, mean, 0.1 + 0.2]
@caption Arithmetic, comparisons, a declared NonFiniteFloat and printing
@value [6.0, -1.5, true, 1.0e200, 2.0, 0.30000000000000004]
@end

@example accept
fn kind(a: Float64, b: Float64) -> NonFiniteResult:
    a / b:
        on NonFiniteFloat {result}:
            return result
    NonFiniteResult::NotANumber

one = 1.0
zero = 0.0
[one.kind(zero), zero.kind(zero), one.kind(-1.0e-320)]
@caption Which non-finite result each division had
@value [NonFiniteResult::PositiveInfinity, NonFiniteResult::NotANumber, NonFiniteResult::NegativeInfinity]
@end

@example reject
x = 1.5
x + 1
@caption No implicit conversion
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 2:1
@end

@example reject
x = 1.0e300
x * 1.0e300
@caption A failure decided while compiling
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 2:1
@end

@topic concept.decimal-rounded
title: DecimalRounded: a decimal-rounding guarantee, not a representation
summary: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N}: a number whose type guarantees that rounding it to N places (half to even) gives the intended decimal, proven by the compiler, observed at N places.
index: concepts
alias: decimal rounded; DecimalRounded; minDecimalsPreserved; maxDecimalsPreserved; decimal places; money; currency; cents; precision guarantee; rounding guarantee; banker's rounding; round half to even; UnrepresentableDecimal
keywords: decimal, rounding, round, half even, precision, places, guarantee, refinement, money, Float64, from_decimal_rounded, quantize, exact, ExactDecimal, observation, ceiling, to_string, text, display
related: concept.scalar-refinements concept.floats stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::to_string task.number-text stdlib.module.std::decimal_rounded error.decimal-precision error.decimal-guarantee-lost error.refinement-parameter error.equality error.known-error concept.static-compile
docs: DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md FLOAT64-MILESTONE-1.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC EQUALITY | *of a DecimalRounded value needs the precision of its type*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *DecimalRounded*

== WHAT IT IS
A value of DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N} stands for a
mathematically intended number t. Its type guarantees that rounding the
value to N decimal places -- or to any fewer -- gives exactly the decimal
that rounding t gives. The rounding rule is round to nearest, ties to
even, of the exact value (1.245 is 1.24 at two places and 1.255 is 1.26),
the same on every target.

    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}
    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2, maxDecimalsPreserved: 4}
    DecimalRounded{
        minDecimalsPreserved: 2
        maxDecimalsPreserved: 4
    }

It is a guarantee, not a representation: not decimal floating point, not
fixed point, not an exact rational. Today a value is held as a binary64
the compiler chooses to satisfy the guarantee; that choice is the
compiler's and may change. The parameters are part of the type, a scalar
refinement written in braces (never angle brackets, and not a generic):

  * minDecimalsPreserved (required, 0 to 1000): the places every
    observation may rely on.
  * maxDecimalsPreserved (optional, at least minDecimalsPreserved): an
    observation ceiling -- no behavior may depend on finer distinctions.
    Every observation reads minDecimalsPreserved places, so the ceiling
    holds by construction; as a type fact it is never dropped or raised.

A Float64 (concept.floats) is a concrete finite binary64 whose every
digit is observable. A DecimalRounded value is a decimal intent known to N
places, and the digits of its representation beyond them are never
observable. An exact decimal type (a future ExactDecimal) would be exact;
DecimalRounded is not.

== RULES
  * A decimal literal is a DecimalRounded constant exactly where a
    DecimalRounded type is expected: a declared result (its final
    expression, an `if` branch, a `return`), an argument of such a
    parameter, a struct field declared as one, a List element where a List
    of them is expected, a handler's value where the handled call returns
    one, and an operand beside a DecimalRounded operand. There it means its
    exact source decimal (0.295, never the binary64 nearest it). Anywhere
    else a decimal literal is a Float64. There are no typed local
    bindings: `x = 0.5` is a Float64.
  * The compiler proves a constant -- a decimal literal, or + - * / and
    unary minus of decimal constants. It computes, with exact arithmetic
    (never host floating point), both the exact result and the binary64 a
    run computes, and accepts the constant at N places only when the run's
    value rounds right at every place up to N; otherwise the constant is
    DECIMAL-PRECISION ("this result is only proven correct to 16 decimal
    places, but ... requires DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 17}").
    A run performs exactly the proven operations: folding and running
    never differ.
  * Arithmetic on any other DecimalRounded value (a parameter, a call
    result, a field) proves nothing and is rejected,
    DECIMAL-GUARANTEE-LOST: only its guarantee is known, and an operand
    anywhere in its rounding cell can carry the exact result across a
    rounding boundary. Unary minus is exact and keeps the type. To compute,
    decay to Float64 and round the result back.
  * A non-finite binary64 result is NonFiniteFloat -- for a constant,
    decided at compile time (KNOWN-ERROR) -- never a precision loss; a
    finite quotient of an exactly zero decimal divisor is DECIMAL-PRECISION.
  * < <= > >= compare round(a, P) with round(b, P), P the smaller of the
    two operands' guaranteed places; == means the same decimal at P and !=
    its negation. At two places 1.235 == 1.244, and neither is less.
  * Equality that does not know the precision is refused: == of a List or
    struct holding DecimalRounded values and an ImmutableSet of them are
    TYPE errors; the type-blind == or hash of an untyped function stops
    with EQUALITY at run time.
  * Weakening is subtyping: a value guaranteed to 4 places stands where 2
    are required, and a value without a ceiling where a ceiling is, never
    the reverse. Nothing converts from or to Float64 implicitly.
  * std::float64::from_decimal_rounded(x) decays to the Float64 nearest
    round(x, N). std::decimal_rounded::round[T](f) mints T from a Float64 by
    rounding f's exact value to T's places; it fails with
    UnrepresentableDecimal when no representation holds that decimal.
  * std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x) (import std::decimal_rounded; also
    x.to_string()) is the guaranteed decimal as text, which ordinary code
    shows directly: round(x, N) half to even with exactly N fraction
    digits (trailing zeros kept, no point at N = 0), "-" only before a
    nonzero result (no negative zero), never a digit of the representation
    -- 0.295 at two places is "0.30". N is the argument's static type's,
    written into the call by the compiler (a value carries none); an
    argument of another type is TYPE, and one whose type is erased is
    refused when the call runs.
  * A program's printed result shows a DecimalRounded value the same way,
    as round(v, N) with exactly N places, N its static type's guarantee
    (main.tcl, every backend). Where the static type does not say N (a
    List mixing kinds), and in a standalone executable's printed result,
    it shows `<DecimalRounded>`: a print that does not know the precision
    shows no digits (make the text with to_string instead).

== WHY
A decimal guarantee lets a program state what it needs -- cents, a rate
to four places -- without choosing a representation, and turns a lost
digit into a type event the compiler reports instead of a wrong decimal at
run time. Reading every observation at the guaranteed places makes every
correct implementation decide alike, whatever extra bits it carries.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn tax_rate() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    0.19

fn total() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    19.99 + 5.01

fn cheaper(a: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, b: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> bool:
    a < b

{rate: tax_rate(), total: total(), cheaper: 1.234.cheaper(1.235), same_cent: 1.235.cheaper(1.244)}
@caption Constants proven to two places; comparisons decide by the two-place decimals
@value {cheaper: true, rate: 0.19, same_cent: false, total: 25.00}
@end

@example accept
import std::float64
import std::decimal_rounded

fn price() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    19.99

fn with_tax(p: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} errors NonFiniteFloat, UnrepresentableDecimal:
    gross = std::float64::from_decimal_rounded(p) * 1.19
    std::decimal_rounded::round[DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}](gross)

price().with_tax():
    on NonFiniteFloat:
        0.00
    on UnrepresentableDecimal:
        0.00
@caption Computing with a parameter: decay to Float64, round back to a guarantee
@value 23.79
@end

@example accept
import std::decimal_rounded
import std::str

fn line(amount: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> str:
    "amount ".concat(amount.to_string())

[line(0.295), line(25.0), line(-0.004)]
@caption The guaranteed decimal as text: exactly two places, the half to even, no negative zero
@value ["amount 0.30", "amount 25.00", "amount 0.00"]
@end

@example reject
fn third() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 17}:
    1.0 / 3.0

third()
@caption 1.0 / 3.0 is correct to 16 places in binary64, not 17
@error CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-PRECISION
@at 2:5
@end

@example reject
fn sum(a: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, b: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    a + b

0.25.sum(0.5)
@caption Arithmetic on values known only by their guarantee proves nothing
@error CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-GUARANTEE-LOST
@at 2:5
@end

@topic concept.scalar-refinements
title: Scalar refinements: compiler-known facts in braces
summary: A scalar refinement, written TYPE{param: N, ...}, adds facts the compiler proves to a type without changing its values: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N} and List[T]{length: N} are the families.
index: concepts
alias: scalar refinement; scalar refinements; refinement parameters; refinement in braces; refinement family
keywords: refinement, braces, parameter, fact, guarantee, family, length, minDecimalsPreserved, decay, forget, subtype, join
related: concept.decimal-rounded concept.list-length concept.refinements concept.integer-domains error.refinement-parameter proof.types
docs: DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md LIST-CARDINALITY-REFINEMENTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
A scalar refinement is a type of a compiler-known family with integer
parameters, written in braces after the type it refines:

    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}    a decimal guarantee
    List[int]{length: 4}                       an exact length

The parameters are part of the type and the compiler owns their meaning.
A refinement strengthens what is known about a value; it never changes what
the value is: a List[int]{length: 4} is an ordinary List[int], with the
same representation on every backend and no tag or check at run time.

== RULES
  * Forgetting is always valid and free: a refined value stands wherever
    its family's weaker type is expected (a List[T]{length: 4} where a
    List[T] is), with no conversion.
  * Recovering a fact takes a proof (a literal, a guard, a declared contract
    the compiler checks) or an explicit, fallible validation
    (std::decimal_rounded::round, std::list::with_length). Admission never
    inserts a check.
  * Each parameter has an order: minDecimalsPreserved is a lower bound (4
    places imply 2), an exact length implies only itself. Two branches
    keep what both state and forget the rest.
  * A family's parameters are checked where the type is written
    (REFINEMENT-PARAMETER). These are not the predicate refinements of
    `refined type` (concept.refinements), and not integer domains
    (concept.integer-domains).

== WHY
One mechanism carries every compiler-known scalar fact, so a new kind of
guarantee needs a schema row, not a type system of its own, and every fact
decays, joins and prints the same way.

== MORE
concept.decimal-rounded, concept.list-length;
DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md, LIST-CARDINALITY-REFINEMENTS.md.

@topic concept.integer-domains
title: Integer domains: `type Name = Int in LO..HI`
summary: A source-defined integer domain is a nominal subset of Int (an interval or a finite set); a value enters it only where the compiler proves the range, through comparisons or a converter function.
index: concepts
alias: integer domain; bounded integer; ranged integer; int subrange
keywords: type, in, interval, range, Byte, domain, converter, subset, refinement
related: concept.integers concept.refinements concept.types proof.ranges rule.no-callable-types error.type
docs: SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md ELIMINATE-AUTOMATIC-TYPE-PREDICATES.md BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md

== WHAT IT IS
    type Digit = Int in 0..9          an inclusive interval
    type Parity = Int in {0, 1}       a finite set

A top-level declaration of a program or module. A Digit value is an
ordinary Int at run time; the type is a statically proven fact about it.
stdlib/byte.bot declares std::byte::Byte this way.

== RULES
  * A value is admitted where a Digit is required only if its range is
    proven inside 0..9: a literal, arithmetic on proven ranges, or a guard
    such as `if 0 <= n and n <= 9:` or `if n < 0 or n > 9: return ...`.
  * Otherwise write a converter: an ordinary function whose declared
    result is the domain, failing with a declared error when out of range.
    Its result annotation is checked like any other.
  * A domain is not callable: `Digit(3)` and `Digit?(n)` are unknown names
    (rule.no-callable-types). No conversion is implicit.

== WHY
A domain records a range the compiler has proven, so a function taking a
Digit never re-checks it. Making entry explicit (a guard or a converter)
keeps the proof visible where the value is produced.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
type Digit = Int in 0..9

error OutOfRange

fn digit(n: int) -> Digit errors OutOfRange:
    if n < 0 or n > 9:
        fail OutOfRange
    n

fn doubled(d: Digit) -> int:
    d * 2

doubled(digit(7))
@caption A converter; the call is proven not to fail, so needs no handler
@value 14
@end

@example reject
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn doubled(d: Digit) -> int:
    d * 2

fn apply(n: int) -> int:
    doubled(n)

0
@caption An unconstrained int is not proven to be a Digit
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 7:13
@end

