compiler derives.

@topic concept.strings
title: Strings and characters
summary: str is an immutable sequence of Unicode scalar values; 'a' is a UnicodeChar, a distinct type; str:: operations need `import std::str`; no formatting, and numbers become text explicitly.
index: concepts
alias: str type; UnicodeChar; character literal; char literal; text values
keywords: string, str, char, character, unicode, scalar, concat, append_char, char_at, substring, length, encode_utf8, escape
related: concept.integers concept.types principle.characters-not-strings warning.one-char-string-literal capability.string-formatting task.number-text stdlib.module.std::str stdlib.std::str::concat stdlib.std::str::char_at stdlib.std::str::substring
docs: README.md UNICODE-CHAR-LITERALS.md STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md

== WHAT IT IS
A `str` value is a sequence of Unicode scalar values: `std::str::length`
counts characters, not bytes. A character literal 'a' (escapes '\n',
'\'', '\\') is a `UnicodeChar`: one scalar value, neither an Int nor a
one-character String.

Three different abstractions, not three spellings of one thing:

    str                text: an immutable sequence of Unicode scalar
                       values (the native backends store it as UTF-8)
    UnicodeChar        one Unicode scalar value (a code point other than
                       a surrogate), a type of its own
    List[UnicodeChar]  an addressable, iterable sequence of such values

A UnicodeChar is not a user-perceived character or a grapheme cluster
(`e` plus a combining accent is two), and not a one-character String. It
is comparable to a Go rune, except that it is a type of its own. A String
is not iterable by `loop`; a List of UnicodeChars is.

After `import std::str`: length, concat, append_char, char_at, substring,
lowercase, encode_utf8 (to a List of bytes). `std::char::scalar_value(c)`
(`import std::char`) gives a character's number.

== RULES
  * `==` compares strings; `+` does not concatenate (a run-time TYPE error):
    use `a.concat(b)`, or `a.append_char('!')` for one character.
  * std::str::char_at and std::str::substring fail with declared errors
    (IndexNotFound; LowerUnderrun/UpperOverrun) unless the compiler proves
    the index or slice valid.
  * A one-character String literal draws ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL: a single
    character is written as a character literal.
  * No formatting or interpolation (capability.string-formatting), and no
    implicit number-to-text conversion: std::int::to_string,
    std::decimal_rounded::to_string (task.number-text).

== WHY
Counting scalar values gives the same answers on every backend and never
splits a character. Keeping UnicodeChar separate from Int and String means
a character is never silently used as a number or as text.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

s = "héllo"
[s.length(), s.append_char('!'), s.char_at(1) == 'é']
@caption Length in characters; a character appended
@value [5, "héllo!", true]
@end

@example accept
import std::char

[std::char::scalar_value('A'), 'a' == 'a']
@caption A UnicodeChar's scalar value
@value [65, true]
@end

== MORE
UNICODE-CHAR-LITERALS.md; stdlib.module.std::str lists every operation.

@topic concept.booleans
title: Booleans and conditions
summary: bool has the two values true and false; `and`, `or` and `not` are short-circuit conditions, and every condition and operand of them must be a Bool -- there is no truthiness.
index: concepts
alias: booleans; bool type; boolean logic; truthiness
keywords: true, false, and, or, not, condition, NOT-BOOLEAN, short-circuit, predicate
related: concept.if-expressions concept.refinements principle.predicate-naming principle.options-are-flags warning.many-boolean-arguments error.not-boolean
docs: README.md BRANCH-FORM-BOOLEAN-LOWERING.md

== WHAT IT IS
`true` and `false` are the values of type `bool`. `a and b`, `a or b` and
`not a` are conditions, not calls: `and` and `or` evaluate their right
operand only when needed. `==` and `!=` compare two values.

== RULES
  * An `if` condition and every operand of `and`, `or`, `not` must be a
    Bool; anything else (an Int, a List) fails with NOT-BOOLEAN when the
    condition is evaluated.
  * Predicates conventionally end in `?` (`empty?`); a function whose
    `true` result proves a refinement declares it (concept.refinements).
  * Passing several boolean literals to one function draws
    MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS: use flag parameters (concept.flags).

== WHY
Without truthiness, a condition always says what it tests (`n != 0`,
`xs.length() > 0`), and the range and refinement facts the compiler reads
from conditions follow the written comparisons exactly.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn in_range?(n: int) -> bool:
    n >= 0 and n < 10

[in_range?(3), in_range?(12), not in_range?(-1)]
@value [true, false, true]
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn first_positive?(xs: List[int]) -> bool:
    xs.length() > 0 and xs.at(0) > 0

[first_positive?([]), first_positive?([4])]
@caption Short-circuit: the guard makes the read provably safe
@value [false, true]
@end

== MORE
concept.if-expressions; proof.ranges for the facts conditions provide.

@topic concept.unit
title: The unit value and type
summary: unit is the one value of the type `unit`: what a function called for its effect returns, the value of an `if` without `else` whose condition is false, and of a bare loop.
index: concepts
alias: unit type; unit value; void
keywords: unit, void, nothing, no value, -> unit, result
related: concept.functions concept.if-expressions concept.loops concept.program-value
docs: README.md

== WHAT IT IS
`unit` is a keyword denoting the unit value; in a type position it names
the unit type (`-> unit`, `List[unit]`, a field `done: unit`). It carries
no information: a function called for its effect returns it.

== RULES
  * `fn log(x) -> unit:` declares a unit result; `return` without a value
    returns unit.
  * An `if` without `else` whose condition is false, a bare `loop:` left by
    `break`, and a destructuring statement evaluate to unit.
  * `-> unit` is also the result of a validator (`-> unit proves x: T`,
    concept.refinements).
  * unit is not null: there is no null or optional value in Botlish
    (capability.null).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn note(n: int) -> unit:
    if n < 0:
        return
    unit

[note(1), note(-1)]
@value [unit, unit]
@end

@example accept
n = 0
x = if n > 0:
    1
x
@caption An if without else: unit when the condition is false
@value unit
@end

== MORE
docs/README.md section 17.

@topic concept.lists
title: Lists: immutable sequences
summary: List[T] is an immutable sequence written [a, b]; its operations need `import std::list`, collecting loops transform it, and it is never destructured positionally.
index: concepts
alias: List type; list literal; immutable list; sequence
keywords: list, List, at, get, append, length, find, any?, all?, IndexNotFound, element, index, empty list
related: concept.loops concept.values concept.destructuring concept.list-length proof.list-bounds rule.no-list-destructuring principle.name-the-parts capability.map-filter task.list-element stdlib.module.std::list stdlib.std::list::at stdlib.std::list::get
docs: README.md STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md MINIMAL-APPLIED-LIST-TYPES.md

== WHAT IT IS
`[1, 2, 3]` is a `List[int]`; `[]` is the empty List. After `import
std::list`: length, at, append (a new List with one more element), get (with
a default), find, any?, all?, none?. Element types are inferred; a parameter
or result may declare `List[T]`, or `List[T]{length: N}` for a List of a
proven exact length (concept.list-length).

== RULES
  * `xs.at(i)` fails with the declared error IndexNotFound unless the
    compiler proves i in range (proof.list-bounds); handle it, admit it,
    or use `xs.get(i, default)`.
  * A List never changes: append returns a new List.
  * Lists are not destructured positionally: `[a, b] = xs` is
    LIST-DESTRUCTURING. A fixed group of different values is a struct
    (principle.name-the-parts).
  * There is no map or filter function: a collecting loop is the
    transformation (`loop x in xs:`), `continue` filters.

== WHY
A List is a sequence of like values. Positional access to a fixed shape
loses the names that make the code readable and the types precise, so
the language steers such data to structs.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

xs = [3, 1, 4]
evens = loop x in xs:
    if x.mod(2) == 1:
        continue
    x
[xs.length(), xs.at(0), xs.get(9, -1), evens]
@caption Indexed read, read with a default, a filtering loop
@value [3, 3, -1, [4]]
@end

@example reject
xs = [1, 2]
[a, b] = xs
a
@caption Lists are not destructured positionally
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:1
@end

