STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md (at and get); RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md.

@topic concept.list-length
title: List[T]{length: N}: a List of a proven exact length
summary: List[T]{length: N} is an ordinary List whose length is proven to be exactly N: it decays to List[T] freely, is minted by proofs or std::list::with_length, and removes bounds and extent checks.
index: concepts
alias: list length refinement; exact length list; fixed length list; List length; length refinement; known length list; list cardinality; LengthMismatch
keywords: List, length, exact, cardinality, refinement, with_length, LengthMismatch, decay, guard, fixed array, extent, bounds, tuple, IndexNotFound, LayoutExtentMismatch
related: concept.scalar-refinements concept.lists stdlib.std::list::with_length stdlib.std::list::length proof.list-bounds concept.binary-layouts principle.name-the-parts warning.fixed-arity-list-return error.refinement-parameter
docs: LIST-CARDINALITY-REFINEMENTS.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *states no exact length*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *element(s) is never one of*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *std::list::with_length*

== WHAT IT IS
`List[int]{length: 4}` is the List family of scalar refinements
(concept.scalar-refinements): a List[int] whose length is proven to be
exactly 4. The length is a non-negative integer literal; it is knowledge,
not representation -- the value is the same List as a List[int], with no
inline array, tuple or fixed-size object behind it, and nothing checks it
at run time.

    fn sum3(r: List[int]{length: 3}) -> int:
        r.at(0) + r.at(1) + r.at(2)      # no IndexNotFound to handle

== RULES
  * Decay is free: a List[T]{length: N} stands wherever a List[T] does.
    The reverse needs the fact: a List[T] argument, field or result where
    a length is required is a TYPE error ("states no exact length"), and a
    List of another length is one too ("is never one of N"): an exact
    length implies no other.
  * What mints it: a List literal (`[1, 2, 3]` is List[int]{length: 3}; `[]`
    has length 0); a declared parameter, struct field or result type;
    std::list::append (one more than its List); a collecting loop with no
    break or continue over a List of known length or a counted range; the
    copy of a fixed layout array (std::binary::parse, std::binary::load,
    std::binary::read: `array 2 of array 3 of E` copies to
    List[List[E]{length: 3}]{length: 2}); a guard `if xs.length() == N:`
    (either operand order, or `!=` with an early return); and
    std::list::with_length[List[T]{length: N}](xs), which checks once and
    fails with LengthMismatch.
  * What does not: `xs.length() >= N` (an exact length is an equality),
    a filtering loop (`continue`), a MutableVector or MutableArray (their
    length can change: they have no length refinement).
  * A declared refined result is a contract the compiler holds every exit
    to, and callers see it. An undeclared result forgets lengths its body
    happens to have, and so do a generic function's instances, handlers'
    admission and mutable containers' element types: an accident of the
    implementation is never an API.
  * Two branches of different lengths join to List[T] (equal lengths keep
    theirs). Nothing is ever specialized by length.
  * The proofs read it: std::list::at with an index in range needs no
    handler (an index out of range is KNOWN-ERROR), std::list::length of it
    is the constant N, and a write of it into a fixed layout array of N
    elements loses its LayoutExtentMismatch path (a List of no known length
    keeps the run-time check).
  * It is not a tuple: a fixed group of different values is still a struct
    (principle.name-the-parts), and FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN warns exactly
    as it would for List[T] (warning.fixed-arity-list-return). A declared
    `-> List[T]{length: N}` is a list result type like `-> List[T]`.

== WHY
Exact lengths were known in several places -- literals, fixed arrays,
loops -- but each proof kept its own copy. As a type, the fact crosses a
function boundary by declaration, is checked once where it enters, and
removes the checks it makes redundant, without making a List anything but
a sequence.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn sum3(r: List[int]{length: 3}) -> int:
    r.at(0) + r.at(1) + r.at(2)

fn total(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    if xs.length() != 3:
        return -1
    xs.sum3()

[total([1, 2, 3]), total([4]), [7, 8, 9].sum3()]
@caption A literal and a length guard mint the fact; the parameter needs no handler
@value [6, -1, 24]
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn row(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]{length: 3} errors LengthMismatch:
    std::list::with_length[List[int]{length: 3}](xs)

fn safe(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]{length: 3}:
    xs.row():
        on LengthMismatch:
            [0, 0, 0]

[safe([1, 2, 3]), safe([1, 2])]
@caption Validated once, explicitly
@value [[1, 2, 3], [0, 0, 0]]
@end

@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::list

layout Row:
    size 3
    align 1
    field cells: array 3 of std::binary::U8 at 0

fn ends(r: List[std::binary::U8]{length: 3}) -> int:
    r.at(0) + r.at(2)

fn total(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> int errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    std::binary::parse(Row, data).cells.ends()

std::binary::layout_length(Row)
@caption A fixed array's copy states its extent
@value 3
@end

@example reject
import std::list

fn sum3(r: List[int]{length: 3}) -> int:
    r.at(0) + r.at(1) + r.at(2)

fn total(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    xs.sum3()

0
@caption A List[int] states no length, and admission inserts no check
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 7:5
@end

@example reject
fn pair() -> List[int]{length: 2}:
    [1, 2, 3]

pair()
@caption A declared length is a contract on every exit
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:8
@end

== MORE
LIST-CARDINALITY-REFINEMENTS.md; concept.scalar-refinements.

@topic concept.structs
title: Structs: named and anonymous records of fields
summary: A struct is a fixed set of named fields: `struct Point:` declares a nominal type built as `Point {x: 1, y: 2}` (every field once), `{x: 1}` is anonymous, `p.x` a static projection.
index: concepts
alias: struct; named struct; anonymous struct; record type; field projection
keywords: struct, field, construction, projection, nominal, anonymous, MISSING-FIELD, UNKNOWN-FIELD, record, immutable
related: concept.destructuring concept.opaque-structs concept.types concept.values concept.error-payloads principle.name-the-parts rule.struct-construction capability.struct-type-import error.missing-field error.unknown-field
docs: STRUCTS.md README.md

== WHAT IT IS
    struct Point:               a nominal type, declared at top level
        x: int
        y: int

    p = Point {x: 1, y: 2}      named construction
    q = {x: 1, y: 2}            an anonymous struct (structural type)
    p.x                         field projection

A module's struct is `geo::Point` (construct `geo::Point {...}`) after
`import geo`. Struct values are immutable and compare with `==` field by
field; anonymous structs with the same fields are the same type whatever
the written order.

== RULES
  * A named construction gives every declared field exactly once
    (MISSING-FIELD, UNKNOWN-FIELD, DUPLICATE-FIELD): there are no default
    values, and each value must be proven admissible for its field type.
  * `value.field` is resolved at compile time to a slot; a receiver not
    known to be a struct with that field is rejected (UNKNOWN-FIELD,
    NOT-A-STRUCT, UNPROVEN-FIELD), never looked up at run time.
  * No field update, spread or methods: build a new value. Functions over
    the struct are ordinary functions, called with method syntax.
  * Anonymous struct types cannot be written in annotations; declare a
    named struct to name the type.
  * A struct may declare type slots, `struct Pair[A, B]:`, and constrain
    one by a trait: concept.parameterized-structs.
  * A struct of another module is spelled qualified; `import type` does not
    import structs (capability.struct-type-import).

== WHY
Named fields say what each part of a value means, where a List of mixed
values would rely on positions (principle.name-the-parts). Static
projection makes `p.x` as cheap as a slot read on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

fn moved(p: Point, dx: int) -> Point:
    Point {x: p.x + dx, y: p.y}

p = Point {x: 1, y: 2}.moved(3)
[p.x, p == Point {y: 2, x: 4}, {a: 1, b: 2} == {b: 2, a: 1}]
@caption Construction, projection and structural equality
@value [4, true, true]
@end

@example reject
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

p = Point {x: 1}
p.x
@caption Every declared field must be given
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD
@at 5:5
@end

== MORE
STRUCTS.md; concept.destructuring to bind several fields at once.

@topic concept.parameterized-structs
title: Parameterized structs: type slots, and slots constrained by a trait
summary: `struct Map[K satisfies Hashable, V]:` is a type constructor whose applications are types; a function over `Map[K, V]` learns K satisfies Hashable by imprinting.
index: concepts
alias: parameterized struct; generic struct; type slot; type parameter; satisfies; type constructor; struct type arguments; Map[K, V]
keywords: slot, satisfies, Hashable, imprint, application, type constructor, constraint, obligation, generic, K, V, TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED, IMPRINT
related: concept.structs concept.traits concept.hashable error.imprint error.trait-not-satisfied error.type
docs: TRAIT-CONSTRAINED-IMPRINTED-TYPES.md STRUCTS.md C-ABI-TYPES.md

== WHAT IT IS
    struct Pair[A, B]:                      slots A and B
        first: A
        second: B

    struct Map[K satisfies Hashable, V]:    K must satisfy Hashable
        keys: MutableArray[K]
        vals: MutableArray[V]

    fn get(m: Map[K, V], key: K) -> V       an ordinary imprinted function

A struct with slots is a type constructor: its types are its applications,
`Pair[int, str]`, one type per slot. Its fields name the slots, and an
application's fields are the declaration's with every slot replaced:
`Map[str, int].keys` is a `MutableArray[str]`. Construct one with the
application written, `Pair[int, str] {first: 1, second: "x"}`, or let the
field values establish it, `Pair {first: 1, second: "x"}`.

A slot declared `K satisfies TRAIT` makes `Map[X, Y]` a valid type only
when X satisfies the trait -- by the ordinary trait satisfaction, so an
intrinsic trait (Hashable) and a trait of a module both work. There is no
generic function declaration: `get` is imprinted, exactly as `fn
first(xs: List[T]) -> T` is. Matching its argument's `Map[str, int]`
against `Map[K, V]` establishes K and V, and because every valid Map
application has a Hashable first argument, the body knows that K
satisfies Hashable: it may hash a K (the total hash) and compare two
(total equality), and call a trait's operations on a K. K stays K -- a
type a `List[K]`, a field and a result may hold -- never a trait view.

== RULES
  * A slot is named like an imprint variable (T, K, V, T2), at most once,
    and is constrained by at most one trait: `satisfies TRAIT`, never `:`,
    `where`, `requires` or an expression.
  * An application gives one type per slot (TYPE otherwise); the bare
    constructor `Pair` is no type. A slot takes an unrestricted concrete
    type: no trait, context trait or affine type.
  * An invalid application -- a constrained slot's argument that does not
    satisfy the trait (a function for Hashable, `any`) -- is
    TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED where it is written: a parameter, a result, a
    field, a List element, a construction, an explicit imprint.
  * A declaration states the obligations of the applications its fields
    hold: `struct Outer[T]: inner: Map[T, int]` is rejected,
    `struct Outer[T satisfies Hashable]` accepted.
  * A function learns obligations only from the applications of its own
    signature, its parameters and its result; its body adds none. A
    constraint never establishes a variable: a result-only K still needs
    explicit imprints, `empty[str, int]()`.
  * Applications are invariant in every slot: a `Box[str]` is no
    `Box[int]`, and two different applications have no join.
  * A function applying a source trait's operation to its constrained
    variable is specialized once per binding (as a trait-polymorphic
    function is per witness), so it is top-level only
    (TRAIT-NESTED-POLYMORPHIC); Hashable's operations need no
    specialization.
  * At run time an application is its constructor's struct value: no
    type arguments, no witness, no check.

== WHY
A hash table needs keys whose equality and hash are total; the type that
requires it is the table, so the table's declaration says so once, and
every function over a table learns it by matching the table's type. The
key type itself is kept, so the table's storage is a `MutableArray[K]`,
not erased to `any`, and a String-keyed instance compares Strings.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
struct H[K satisfies Hashable]:
    key: K

fn fingerprint(h: H[K]) -> int:
    h.key.hash()

fn same(h: H[K], other: K) -> bool:
    h.key == other

[H {key: "xy"}.fingerprint() == "xy".hash(), H[int] {key: 3}.same(3)]
@caption The body may hash and compare a K: K satisfies Hashable because H's slot requires it
@value [true, true]
@end

@example reject
struct H[K satisfies Hashable]:
    key: K

fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

h = H {key: twice}
1
@caption A function is no argument for a Hashable slot: the application H[Fn{...}] is invalid
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
@at 7:5
@end

@example reject
struct Map[K satisfies Hashable, V]:
    keys: List[K]
    vals: List[V]

struct Outer[T]:
    inner: Map[T, int]

1
@caption Outer's T does not state the Hashable its field's application requires
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
@at 6:12
@end

