how a function's variables are established.

@topic concept.opaque-structs
title: Opaque structs: representation owned by one module
summary: `opaque struct T:` is an ordinary struct whose fields only the declaring module may construct or inspect; everywhere else a T is a whole value to pass, store, compare and hash.
index: concepts
alias: opaque struct; opaque type; abstract data type; encapsulation
keywords: opaque, representation, module, construct, inspect, authority, OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION, OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION, Bytes
related: concept.structs concept.modules concept.contexts concept.private rule.opaque-authority error.opaque-construction error.opaque-representation
docs: OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    opaque struct Token:
        value: int

The declaring module (the file) is the only code that may write `Token
{value: ...}`, read `t.value` or destructure a Token. Other code holds
Tokens as values and uses the module's functions. std::abi::bytes::Bytes and
std::linux::io::LinuxIO are opaque structs.

== RULES
  * Outside the owner, construction is OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION and projection
    or destructuring is OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION; both are static checks with
    no run-time test.
  * Authority is the declaring module exactly: no import, parent or child
    module grants it. An entry program owns its own opaque structs.
  * `==`, `!=` and `hash` work on whole values everywhere; an opaque value
    prints as `<opaque NS::T>`.
  * `opaque` combines with `context` (`opaque context struct`).
  * Opacity is per struct and says who may inspect a representation;
    who may name a declaration at all is `private` (concept.private), an
    independent modifier: `private opaque struct` is both.

== WHY
It is representation ownership, not object-oriented privacy: the module
that defines a value's invariants is the only code that can create or take
apart its representation, so the invariants cannot be bypassed. The
compiler still sees the fields, so opacity costs nothing at run time.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::str

a = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("hi"))
b = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("hi"))
[a == b, std::abi::bytes::length(a).value]
@caption Bytes values are compared whole and used through their module
@value [true, 2]
@end

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::str

b = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("hi"))
b.storage
@caption Another module's opaque fields cannot be read
@error CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION
@at 5:3
@end

