OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md.

@topic concept.private
title: Private declarations: `private` makes a declaration module-local
summary: `private fn`, `private struct`, ... -- a top-level declaration only its own module may name or use; `private` decides who may access a declaration, `opaque` who may inspect a representation.
index: concepts
alias: private; public; visibility; export; hide function; module privacy; private helper
keywords: private, public, module, access, visibility, helper, opaque, PRIVATE-ACCESS, PRIVATE-TYPE-ESCAPE, airlock
related: concept.opaque-structs concept.modules concept.contexts concept.direct-modules error.private-access error.private-type-escape warning.context-resource-escape
docs: MODULE-PRIVACY-AND-CONTEXT-ESCAPE-MILESTONE-1.md OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md IMPORTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    private fn renderer(context sdl: Sdl) -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[SDL_Renderer]:
        ...

`private` before a top-level declaration -- a function, a module binding,
a type, an error, a struct, an enum, a trait, a layout, an extern c fn --
makes it accessible only to code of the module that declares it. Every
other declaration is public, as before; there is no `public` keyword.

== RULES
  * Written first, once, on a top-level declaration only: `private opaque
    struct`, `private nomethod fn`, `private context trait`. Nested in a
    function it is a syntax error: privacy is module-local, never lexical.
  * Another module naming a private declaration -- qualified, rooted,
    with `import type`, as a value, a type, a construction, an error or an
    enum's case -- is PRIVATE-ACCESS. So is method syntax `x.f()` that
    finds only a private `f` of an imported module: a private function is
    no method candidate there, and competes with nothing.
  * A private function implements no trait or context-trait requirement:
    an implementation is public interface.
  * A public declaration may not expose a private type or error in its
    interface (parameters, declared or inferred result, context
    requirements, errors, a non-opaque struct's fields, a trait's
    requirements): PRIVATE-TYPE-ESCAPE. An opaque struct may hold one as
    hidden representation.
  * Access is decided by the module's identity, its file's namespace, never
    by a spelling. The entry program is imported by nothing, so `private`
    there changes nothing.
  * Nothing at run time: a private function is compiled, called and linked
    like any other.

== WHY
`private` determines who may access a declaration; `opaque` who may
inspect a representation (concept.opaque-structs). A module that wraps raw
foreign authority behind a context -- an airlock -- needs helpers that take
and return pointers or native views; private, they are implementation, not
API, and the compiler can tell (warning.context-resource-escape).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import gfx

gfx::ready()
@module gfx
import std::ffi::c

private fn renderer() -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]:
    std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()

fn ready() -> bool:
    r = renderer()
    not std::ffi::c::null?(r)
@caption A private helper may return a raw pointer: only gfx can call it
@value false
@end

@example accept
import gfx

s = gfx::open(7)
gfx::area(s)
@module gfx
opaque struct Surface:
    width: int

fn open(width: int) -> Surface:
    Surface {width: width}

fn area(s: Surface) -> int:
    s.width * s.width
@caption A public opaque value: every module may hold one, only gfx looks inside
@value 49
@end

@example accept
import gfx

with context gfx::create()
gfx::draw(3)
@module gfx
private struct State:
    scale: int

opaque context struct Gfx:
    state: State

fn create() -> Gfx:
    Gfx {state: State {scale: 10}}

private fn scale(context g: Gfx) -> int:
    g.state.scale

fn draw(x: int, context g: Gfx) -> int:
    x * scale()
@caption A public contextual operation; its state and helper stay private
@value 30
@end

@example reject
import gfx

gfx::scale(2)
@module gfx
private fn scale(x: int) -> int:
    x * 10
@caption Another module may not call a private function
@error SURFACE MODULE PRIVATE-ACCESS
@at 3:1
@end

