@topic concept.destructuring
title: Struct destructuring: `{a, b: c} = value`
summary: `{user, expires: expiry} = s` binds struct fields by name (renaming, omitting and nesting allowed); Lists are deliberately not destructured positionally.
index: concepts
alias: struct destructuring; destructuring; unpack struct; destructure
keywords: destructure, pattern, fields, rename, nested, bind, LIST-DESTRUCTURING, unpack, tuple
related: concept.structs concept.bindings concept.error-payloads concept.lists rule.no-list-destructuring principle.name-the-parts error.list-destructuring
docs: STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md MULTI-VALUE-RESULTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    {user, expires: expiry} = session

evaluates `session` once, then binds `user` to `session.user` and `expiry`
to `session.expires`, exactly as separate bindings would. `{field}` is
`{field: field}`; the left name is the field, the right name the new
binding. A nested pattern destructures a field in turn:
`{point: {x, y}, label} = r`. Named and anonymous structs work alike.

== RULES
  * Every requested field must exist statically (the projection's
    diagnostics apply); selecting a field twice or binding a name twice is
    rejected.
  * It is not pattern matching: no defaults, wildcards, rest/spread or
    assignment to existing names, and it cannot fail at run time.
  * Legal where an ordinary binding is (not at a module's top level). The
    statement itself evaluates to unit.
  * `[a, b] = xs` is LIST-DESTRUCTURING: return a struct with named fields
    instead of a fixed-size List.
  * Handlers destructure error payloads the same way: `on E {field}:`.

== WHY
A function that returns several values returns a struct, and the caller
takes it apart by name, so the parts cannot be confused by position.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
struct Split:
    head: int
    rest: List[int]

s = Split {head: 1, rest: [2, 3]}
{head, rest: tail} = s
r = {point: {x: 3, y: 4}, label: "p1"}
{point: {x, y}, label} = r
[head, tail, x + y]
@caption Renaming, nesting and anonymous structs
@value [1, [2, 3], 7]
@end

@example reject
struct Split:
    head: int

s = Split {head: 1}
{tail} = s
tail
@caption A field the type does not have
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FIELD
@at 5:2
@end

