@topic concept.enums
title: Enums: closed sets of named cases
summary: `enum Color:` declares a nominal type with payload-free cases written `Color::Red`; cases compare with == and hash, and have no number, String form, ordering or bare-name visibility.
index: concepts
alias: enum; enumeration; enum case; enumerated type
keywords: enum, case, nominal, closed, ==, hash, import type, ordinal, match, switch, UNKNOWN-ENUM-CASE
related: concept.structs concept.imports concept.types rule.import-type capability.match error.unknown-enum-case
docs: ENUMS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    enum Light:
        Red,
        Amber,
        Green,

declares the type `Light` and three distinct values `Light::Red`,
`Light::Amber`, `Light::Green`. A module's enum is `geo::Light` (cases
`geo::Light::Red`) after `import geo`, or `Light::Red` after
`import type geo::Light`.

== RULES
  * A case is always qualified by its enum: a bare `Red` is unbound, and
    `Light::Red()` or `Light::Red {}` are rejected (a case is a value, not
    a constructor).
  * Two enums are unrelated even with identical cases.
  * No ordinal, Int or String conversion, no ordering, no case list or
    count, no payloads (yet). Comparisons are `==` and `!=`.
  * There is no `match` or `switch`: branch with `if`/`elif` on `==`
    (capability.match).

== WHY
An enum is a closed set of names, not a disguised integer: code cannot
depend on case order or numbering, so cases can be added or reordered
without silent changes, and the compiler keeps the full case set for
future exhaustive matching.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
enum Light:
    Red,
    Amber,
    Green,

fn next(l: Light) -> Light:
    if l == Light::Red:
        return Light::Green
    if l == Light::Green:
        return Light::Amber
    Light::Red

[next(Light::Red), next(Light::Amber) == Light::Red]
@value [Light::Green, true]
@end

@example reject
enum Light:
    Red,
    Green,

Red
@caption A case is never visible by its bare name
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 5:1
@end

