@topic concept.refinements
title: Refinement types: `refined type` and `proves`
summary: `refined type T = Carrier` names a property that only the owning module's predicates and validators (`proves x: T`) establish; the compiler tracks it through control flow.
index: concepts
alias: refinement type; refined type; proves clause; proof-producing predicate; validator function
keywords: refined, proves, predicate, validator, carrier, Emailish, proof, flow, narrowing, validate_
related: concept.integer-domains concept.types concept.booleans proof.refinements warning.proves-naming principle.predicate-naming rule.no-callable-types error.type
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md CROSS-MODULE-REFINED-SIGNATURES.md ELIMINATE-AUTOMATIC-TYPE-PREDICATES.md

== WHAT IT IS
    refined type NonEmpty = str

    fn non_empty?(s: str) -> bool proves s: NonEmpty:      a predicate
    fn validate_non_empty(s: str) -> unit proves s: NonEmpty errors Empty:
                                                           a validator

After `if non_empty?(s):` the then-branch knows `s` is a NonEmpty; after
`validate_non_empty(s)` completes, `s` is a NonEmpty for the rest of the
path. A refined value is its carrier everywhere a carrier is expected.

== RULES
  * A value becomes T only where a proof-producing function of T's own
    module has proven it; nothing converts implicitly, and a refinement is
    never checked at run time.
  * The proof clause names one parameter, follows `-> bool` or `-> unit`,
    precedes `errors`, and is allowed only in the module declaring T.
  * Facts flow through `and`, `or`, `not`, and a Bool binding
    (`ok = non_empty?(s)` then `if ok:`); they survive an `if` when every
    branch that completes proves them.
  * Naming: predicates end in `?`, validators are `validate` or start with
    `validate_` (PROVES-NAMING warns otherwise).
  * A refined value has exactly its carrier's representation: no tag,
    wrapper or check exists at run time.

== WHY
Validation happens once, where it is written, and the type carries the
result to every function that needs it; a function requiring a NonEmpty
cannot be called with an unchecked string.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

refined type NonEmpty = str

fn non_empty?(s: str) -> bool proves s: NonEmpty:
    std::str::length(s) > 0

fn shout(s: NonEmpty) -> str:
    s.append_char('!')

fn safe(s: str) -> str:
    if non_empty?(s):
        return shout(s)
    "(empty)"

[safe("hi"), safe("")]
@caption A predicate proves the refinement in the then-branch
@value ["hi!", "(empty)"]
@end

@example reject
import std::str

refined type NonEmpty = str

fn shout(s: NonEmpty) -> str:
    s.append_char('!')

shout("hi")
@caption A plain str is not a NonEmpty until a proof says so
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 8:7
@end

