@topic concept.functions
title: Functions: declarations, parameters, results and errors
summary: `fn name(params) -> Result errors E1, E2:` declares a function whose body's last expression is its value; parameters and the result may be typed, and the errors clause lists what a call may fail with.
index: concepts
alias: function declaration; fn keyword; return statement; result type; parameter list
keywords: fn, function, parameter, argument, return, result, errors, recursion, arity, typed parameter, default argument, ARITY
related: concept.closures concept.function-types concept.flags concept.contexts concept.errors concept.method-sugar concept.nomethod concept.specialization concept.definition-order rule.typed-parameters principle.small-functions error.arity
docs: README.md STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn clamp(n: int, low: int, high: int) -> int errors Inverted:
        if low > high:
            fail Inverted
        if n < low:
            return low
        if n > high:
            return high
        n

A declaration binds the name (at top level, in a module or nested in
another function). The body's last expression is the result; `return e`
leaves early (`return` alone returns unit). After the ordinary parameters
a function may declare flags (concept.flags) and context parameters
(concept.contexts).

== RULES
  * Arity is exact: no default arguments and no keyword arguments other
    than flags; a variadic function (concept.variadics) takes its fixed
    arguments and any number of trailing ones. A call with the wrong
    number of arguments fails with ARITY (statically for a function with
    flags or a variadic section; for other functions the check is
    currently made when the call runs).
  * Postfix binds tighter than unary minus: `-3.clamp(0, 10)` is
    `-(3.clamp(0, 10))`; write `(-3).clamp(0, 10)`.
  * Typed parameters and results are contracts proven at every call and
    every exit (concept.types); untyped ones make the function generic.
  * The `errors` clause lists the declared errors a call may complete with;
    each error the body can raise must be handled or listed there
    (concept.errors). Without the clause the function fails with nothing.
  * A function may call itself; it may not call a function defined later
    (concept.definition-order).
  * A function is a value: it can be bound (`g = f`), passed and returned
    (concept.function-types), unless it declares flags or a context.
  * Deep non-tail recursion is limited by the interpreter's stack (TCL
    LIMIT STACK on interp); self tail calls run in constant stack on the
    compile and native backends.

== WHY
Signatures are where a function states what it needs and what can go
wrong; the compiler proves both, so callers can rely on them without
reading the body.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
error Inverted

fn clamp(n: int, low: int, high: int) -> int errors Inverted:
    if low > high:
        fail Inverted
    if n < low:
        return low
    if n > high:
        return high
    n

[15.clamp(0, 10), (-3).clamp(0, 10)]
@caption Typed parameters, early returns, a declared error
@value [10, 0]
@end

@example reject
fn half_label(n: int) -> str:
    n

half_label(4)
@caption The declared result is a contract the body must prove
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:14
@end

== MORE
docs/README.md section 17; concept.closures for nested functions.

@topic concept.closures
title: Closures: nested named functions
summary: A function declared inside another captures the bindings it can see and is an ordinary value; there is no anonymous function (lambda) expression.
index: concepts
alias: closure; closures; nested function; captured variable; inner function
keywords: closure, capture, nested, fn, callback, lambda, anonymous, environment, higher-order, function value
related: concept.functions concept.function-types concept.definition-order capability.lambdas concept.mutable-vector error.mutable-place-receiver
docs: README.md STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md

== WHAT IT IS
A `fn` declared in a function body is a closure: it sees the enclosing
parameters and the bindings defined before it, and can be called, bound,
passed as an argument or returned.

== RULES
  * There is no anonymous function expression: `fn` starts a declaration
    only, so `g = fn(y): y` is a syntax error (capability.lambdas). Declare
    a named nested function and pass its name.
  * A closure captures values. Since bindings never change, it sees the
    value each name had where the closure was created.
  * A nested function cannot mutate a MutableVector or MutableArray of its
    enclosing function (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER); pass it in and return
    the result. An affine value (a coroutine handle) cannot be captured.
  * Like any binding, a nested function is visible only after its
    declaration.

== WHY
Named nested functions keep callbacks readable and give the compiler one
binding per function body; with immutable captures a closure is just a
function plus the values it was created with.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn make_adder(x: int):
    fn add(y: int) -> int:
        x + y
    add

add10 = make_adder(10)
add10(32)
@caption add captures x
@value 42
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn has_above?(xs: List[int], limit: int) -> bool:
    fn above?(x: int) -> bool:
        x > limit
    xs.any?(above?)

[[3, 12].has_above?(10), [3].has_above?(10)]
@caption A nested predicate passed to std::list::any?
@value [true, false]
@end

