for typing a callback parameter.

@topic concept.mutual-recursion
title: Mutual recursion: not supported today
summary: Two functions cannot call each other: a function sees only what is defined before it, and the planned `mutual:` group (MUTUAL-RECURSION.md) is a design that is not implemented.
index: concepts
alias: mutual recursion; mutually recursive functions; mutual group
keywords: mutual, recursion, recursive, forward reference, UNBOUND, cycle, is_even, is_odd, mutual:
related: concept.definition-order concept.functions concept.closures rule.forward-references error.unbound
docs: MUTUAL-RECURSION.md STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md

== WHAT IT IS
Because bindings are visible only after their definition
(concept.definition-order), in a pair of functions that call each other
the first one's reference to the second is UNBOUND. A function may call
itself.

== RULES
  * `mutual:` is not part of the language yet: writing it is a syntax
    error (expected end of line, found ":"). MUTUAL-RECURSION.md records
    the design (a module-level group of functions with declared results).
  * Ordinary bindings are never hoisted, so no ordering of two mutually
    recursive functions compiles.

== WHY
Strict definition order fixes what every reference means from the text
before it; an explicit group is the planned way to let a set of functions
see each other without bringing back hoisting.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Merge the functions into one recursive function with a mode argument
    (a bool, an enum, or the state itself).
  * Or pass the later function to the earlier one as a parameter: the
    second function calls the first and hands itself over.
  * For a recursive data walk, one function per data shape usually becomes
    one function that branches on the shape.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn odd_via(n: int, even: Fn{args: [int], return: bool}) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return false
    even(n - 1)

fn is_even(n: int) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return true
    odd_via(n - 1, is_even)

is_even(10)
@caption The later function passes itself to the earlier one
@value true
@end

@example reject
fn is_even(n: int) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return true
    is_odd(n - 1)

fn is_odd(n: int) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return false
    is_even(n - 1)

is_even(10)
@caption is_odd is declared later
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 4:5
@end

== MORE
MUTUAL-RECURSION.md (design, not implemented);
STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md for why nothing is hoisted.

@topic concept.method-sugar
title: Method syntax: `x.f(a)` is `f(x, a)`
summary: `value.f(a, b)` is another spelling of the call f(value, a, b), allowed when a function f is visible by that name; a namespace's functions become visible to method syntax only through `import NS`.
index: concepts
alias: method call; method syntax; receiver syntax; dot call; method-call sugar
keywords: method, receiver, dot, candidate, import, AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL, NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD, MISSING-IMPORT, sugar, chaining, extension method
related: concept.nomethod concept.imports concept.functions rule.method-requires-import rule.method-resolution warning.method-eligible principle.receiver-first error.missing-import error.ambiguous-method-call error.no-applicable-method
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md IMPORTS.md WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md

== WHAT IT IS
`x.f(a)` does not ask what methods `x` has: it asks whether a function
named `f` is visible here and whether the ordinary call `f(x, a)` would be
valid. If so, it is that call -- the same HIR, errors and code. There are
no method declarations, method tables or receiver types. Calls chain:
`n.inc().twice()` is `twice(inc(n))`.

== RULES
  * Visible means: a function or binding in scope at the call (the file's, a
    parameter, a local), a root native (mod, hash, ...), or a member of a
    namespace the file directly imports. Writing `std::list::at(xs, 0)`
    never makes `xs.at(0)` work without `import std::list` (MISSING-IMPORT
    names the import to add).
  * When several distinct visible functions have the name (a local `at`
    and `std::list::at`), each is tried as the ordinary call and exactly one
    must fit: none is NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD, several AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL.
    There is no precedence: write the one you mean as an ordinary call.
  * `x.f` without a call is a field projection, and `(x.f)(a)` calls a
    field. If `f` is both a visible function and a possibly callable field
    of x's struct type, `x.f(a)` is AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL.
  * A `nomethod` function is never a candidate (concept.nomethod).

== WHY
Method syntax applies a function visible by name, and imports are what
decide which names a file can see: making a namespace's functions
candidates only through `import` keeps every dependency in the file's
header and every call resolvable without knowing receiver types.
Receiver-first spelling is the preferred call form
(principle.receiver-first); METHOD-ELIGIBLE points out functional calls
that could use it.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list
import std::mutable_array

xs = [1, 2, 3]
a = std::mutable_array::from_list([4, 5])
[xs.at(0), a.at(1), xs.length()]
@caption Each call resolves to the one candidate whose call is valid
@value [1, 5, 3]
@end

@example reject
xs = [1, 2, 3]
xs.at(0)
@caption Without `import std::list`, std::list::at is not visible
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT
@at 2:4
@end

@example reject
import std::list

fn at(xs: List[int], i: int) -> int:
    0

[1, 2].at(0)
@caption Two visible functions accept the receiver: no precedence
@error CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL
@at 6:8
@end

