@topic concept.nomethod
title: `nomethod fn`: a function whose first parameter is no receiver
summary: `nomethod fn f(...)` declares, as part of f's interface, that its first argument is not a receiver: method syntax never resolves to it (NOMETHOD-CALL), and METHOD-ELIGIBLE never suggests it.
index: concepts
alias: nomethod; nomethod fn; no method syntax
keywords: nomethod, method syntax, receiver, interface, declaration, NOMETHOD-CALL, METHOD-ELIGIBLE, bit_and, suppression
related: concept.method-sugar warning.method-eligible principle.receiver-first error.nomethod-call lint.policy
docs: WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md METHOD-SUGAR.md REFACTOR-WARNINGS-CLEAN.md

== WHAT IT IS
    nomethod fn atan2(y: int, x: int) -> int:

A modifier on one function declaration, accepted wherever `fn` is. It is
the author's statement that receiver syntax would misread the function:
`y.atan2(x)` suggests y is the subject when y and x are of equal standing.

== RULES
  * A method-style call that would resolve to it is NOMETHOD-CALL ("is
    declared nomethod and cannot be called with method syntax"). Method
    syntax skips it as a candidate, so another visible function of the
    same name is still found.
  * Called functionally it is an ordinary function: same type, errors,
    code. Aliases and imports keep the property.
  * Shipped examples: bit_and, bit_or and bit_xor (symmetric operands),
    std::mutable_array::create (the first argument is a length),
    std::mutable_array::copy (the first argument is the destination), and
    stdlib/test.bot's registration helpers (the first argument is a name).

== WHY
It is not a warning suppression: it changes which call forms are legal,
for every caller, as a fact about the function's interface. Botlish has
no call-site lint switches; the function's author decides once whether
its first parameter is a receiver, and METHOD-ELIGIBLE (which suggests
method syntax for functional calls) then stays silent for it.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
nomethod fn distance(a: int, b: int) -> int:
    if a > b:
        return a - b
    b - a

distance(3, 10)
@caption Called functionally, and never suggested as 3.distance(10)
@value 7
@quiet
@end

@example reject
nomethod fn distance(a: int, b: int) -> int:
    if a > b:
        return a - b
    b - a

3.distance(10)
@caption Method syntax to a nomethod function is an error
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOMETHOD-CALL
@at 6:3
@end

