WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md; warning.method-eligible.

@topic concept.flags
title: Flag parameters: `flags :name`
summary: `fn open(path, flags :append):` declares named Boolean options; a call writes `open(p, :append)` to set one, every unnamed flag is false, and a function with flags cannot be passed as a value.
index: concepts
alias: flag parameter; flag parameters; boolean option; named flag
keywords: flags, :name, option, bool, boolean, UNKNOWN-FLAG, DUPLICATE-FLAG, FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE, keyword argument, MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS
related: concept.functions concept.booleans principle.options-are-flags warning.many-boolean-arguments rule.flags error.unknown-flag error.flag-function-value
docs: FLAGS.md WARNINGS-MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn label(n: int, flags :loud, :signed) -> str:

The flag section follows the ordinary parameters. Inside the body each
flag is an immutable Bool binding (`loud`), true exactly when the call
names it: `label(1)`, `label(1, :loud)`, `1.label(:loud, :signed)`.

== RULES
  * A flag is false unless written; there are no defaults, negated,
    required or computed flags, and `:name` is not a value (it exists only
    as a trailing call argument).
  * Calls are checked against the declaration: UNKNOWN-FLAG,
    DUPLICATE-FLAG; flags come after the ordinary arguments.
  * A function with flags can be called or aliased (`g = label`), but not
    passed, returned or stored as a value (FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE).
  * Flags work with method syntax and are not counted as ordinary
    parameters.

== WHY
`open(path, true, false)` does not say what true means; `open(path,
:append)` does. Flags are the language's form for options, and
MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS points at calls that pass several boolean literals
instead (principle.options-are-flags).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn label(n: int, flags :loud, :signed) -> str:
    if loud and signed:
        return "LOUD SIGNED"
    if loud:
        return "LOUD"
    "quiet"

[label(1), label(1, :loud), 1.label(:loud, :signed)]
@value ["quiet", "LOUD", "LOUD SIGNED"]
@end

@example reject
fn label(n: int, flags :loud) -> str:
    if loud:
        return "LOUD"
    "quiet"

label(1, :shout)
@caption Only declared flags may be written
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FLAG
@at 6:10
@end

