@topic concept.variadics
title: Variadic parameters: `variadic NAME: List[T]`
summary: `fn sum(variadic xs: List[int])` takes any number of trailing arguments as one immutable List; `extern c fn f(fixed, variadic)` is a variadic C function.
index: concepts
alias: variadic function; variadic parameter; varargs; variable arguments; rest parameter; C varargs; printf
keywords: variadic, varargs, trailing arguments, List, imprint, extern c fn, va_arg, default argument promotions, MALFORMED-VARIADIC-SECTION
related: concept.functions concept.flags concept.c-ffi error.malformed-variadic-section error.arity error.extern-syntax
docs: VARIADICS-VERTICAL-SLICE-1.md FLAGS.md C-FFI.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn sum(variadic numbers: List[int]) -> int:
    fn log(level: int, variadic parts: List[str]) -> int:

The variadic section is a function's last parameter binding: after the
ordinary parameters, the flags and the context section (only a resume
clause follows it), at most once, `variadic NAME: List[T]` -- or a bare
`variadic NAME: List`, whose element type each call imprints. A call
passes its fixed arguments, then any number of trailing ones, then its
flags: `sum()` is `sum([])`, `sum(1, 2, 3)` is `sum([1, 2, 3])`,
`log(1, "a", "b")` is `log(1, ["a", "b"])`.

== RULES
  * The body receives an ordinary immutable List. There is no variadic
    value, type or run-time representation: the call is an ordinary call
    whose last argument is the List construction, every argument
    evaluated once, in written order.
  * Each trailing argument is proven in the element type by its own proof:
    a `variadic xs: List[Small]` takes `smalls(1, 9)` (where the literal
    `[1, 9]`, a List[int], would not be admitted), and a trailing argument
    outside it is a TYPE error at that argument.
  * A call passes at least the fixed arguments (ARITY, at compile time).
  * Method syntax and aliases collect the trailing arguments too
    (`(2).log("a")`, `g = sum` then `g(1, 2)`). Used as a value, a
    variadic function is the ordinary function of its List parameter: a
    call through the value passes the List itself (`apply(sum, [1, 2])`).
    A List written as a trailing argument is one element: there is no
    spread.
  * A bare `List` makes the function imprinted (an empty call collects
    List[never], or an explicit imprint names the element: `f[int]()`), so
    it is called, never passed as a value. A variadic function implements
    no trait requirement or context-trait operation.
  * C: `extern c fn printf(format: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Char] read
    noescape, variadic) -> std::ffi::c::Int` -- the bare word `variadic`,
    last, after at least one fixed parameter (C's `...`, never written
    `...`). Each trailing argument has exactly one C ABI type: a C integer
    scalar, std::ffi::c::Bool, Float64 (a C double), a std::ffi::c::Ptr or
    an std::ffi::c::FnPtr. A plain Int (which says no C type), a String, a
    List, a struct, byte storage and an erased value are refused. C's
    default argument promotions apply (an integer narrower than int, and
    _Bool, are passed as int). A trailing pointer has no contract, so the
    call is direct authority (concept.direct-modules). The call's machine
    signature is built per call: x86-64 System V (with its %al count of
    vector registers), Linux AArch64 (AAPCS64), Apple AArch64 (every
    trailing argument on the stack, in an 8-byte slot) and Windows x86-64
    (Win64: every argument at its position, each float or double of the
    first four positions in both its xmm register and its position's
    integer register). The Microsoft C runtime defines printf and snprintf
    inline in its headers: a binding of them on Windows declares
    `extern c library "legacy_stdio_definitions"`.

== WHY
A function of "any number of values of one type" is a List parameter whose
caller does not write the brackets. Keeping the body's view an ordinary
List keeps every analysis, ownership rule and backend unchanged; only the
call collects. A C variadic function is a different thing -- each call has
its own C signature -- so its trailing arguments must each say which C type
they are, which a Botlish integer does not.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn sum_from(xs: List[int], i: int, acc: int) -> int:
    if i == xs.length():
        acc
    else:
        x = xs.at(i):
            on IndexNotFound:
                0
        xs.sum_from(i + 1, acc + x)

fn sum(variadic numbers: List[int]) -> int:
    numbers.sum_from(0, 0)

fn weighted(weight: int, variadic numbers: List[int]) -> int:
    weight * numbers.sum_from(0, 0)

[sum(), sum(1, 2, 3), weighted(2, 4, 5), (3).weighted(1)]
@value [0, 6, 18, 3]
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn count(variadic values: List) -> int:
    std::list::length(values)

[count(), count("ab", "cd"), count(true, 2, 3)]
@caption A bare List: each call imprints its element type
@value [0, 2, 3]
@end

@example reject
type Small = Int in 0..9

fn smalls(variadic xs: List[Small]) -> List[Small]:
    xs

smalls(1, 9, 10)
@caption Each trailing argument is proven in the element type
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 6:14
@end

@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::byte
import std::ffi::c
import std::list
import std::str

extern c fn printf(format: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Char] read noescape, variadic) -> std::ffi::c::Int

fn nul() -> std::byte::Byte:
    0

fn cstring(text: str) -> std::abi::bytes::Bytes:
    std::abi::bytes::from_list(text.encode_utf8().append(nul()))

fn c_long(n: std::ffi::c::Long) -> std::ffi::c::Long:
    n

printf(cstring("%ld %.1f\n"), c_long(-7), 2.5)
@caption C's printf: a C long and a double, the characters it wrote
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@value 7
@end

