@topic concept.function-types
title: Function types: `Fn{args, return, errors}` and `Coroutine{...}`
summary: A parameter, result or field may declare Fn{args: [T], return: R, errors: [E]}; any callable whose contract fits is accepted, and calls through it are checked against the contract.
index: concepts
alias: Fn type; function type; callback type; Coroutine type; structural function type
keywords: Fn, Coroutine, callable, callback, higher-order, args, return, errors, contract, function value, typed callback
related: concept.functions concept.closures concept.coroutines concept.errors proof.callable-contracts concept.affine error.type
docs: STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md VERIFIED-CALLABLE-CONTRACT-BOUNDARY.md AFFINE-VALUES.md

== WHAT IT IS
    Fn{args: [int, str], return: bool, errors: [NotFound]}

Named fields in any order; `errors` may be omitted and then means
`errors: []`. Any function or native whose parameters admit the argument
types, whose result fits R and whose possible errors are among E fits the
type. `Coroutine{args: [...], return: R, errors: [...]}` has the same
contract for a coroutine handle (concept.coroutines); the two kinds never
convert into each other.

== RULES
  * Arguments are contravariant, the result covariant, the errors a
    subset: a callable that may fail with an error the type does not list
    is rejected where the value acquires the type (a TYPE error naming the
    error set).
  * A call through an Fn value is typed and error-checked from the
    contract, so its declared errors must be handled like any call's.
  * Functions with flags or a context cannot be function values
    (FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE, CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE).
  * An untyped parameter can also receive a function; its calls are then
    analyzed from what is actually passed (concept.specialization).

== WHY
A declared function type states what a callback may do, including how it
may fail, so the higher-order function can be checked once against the
contract instead of against every callable it might receive.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
error Bad

fn safe_call(x: int, f: Fn{args: [int], return: int, errors: [Bad]}) -> int:
    f(x):
        on Bad:
            0

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if n > 3:
        fail Bad
    n

[2.safe_call(risky), 5.safe_call(risky)]
@caption The contract admits Bad, and the caller handles it
@value [2, 0]
@end

@example reject
error Bad

fn call_clean(x: int, f: Fn{args: [int], return: int}) -> int:
    f(x)

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if n > 3:
        fail Bad
    n

2.call_clean(risky)
@caption risky may fail with Bad, which the type does not allow
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 11:14
@end

