STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md; proof.callable-contracts.

@topic concept.imports
title: Imports: explicit, exact, not transitive
summary: `import NS` allows NS::member names and makes NS's functions method candidates; `import type NS::T` binds one type's short name; imports are exact and never transitive.
index: concepts
alias: import; imports; import statement; qualified name; namespace import
keywords: import, import type, namespace, qualified, ::, header, MISSING-IMPORT, transitive, alias, wildcard, UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
related: concept.modules concept.method-sugar concept.enums rule.imports-not-transitive rule.import-type rule.method-requires-import capability.struct-type-import principle.explicit-dependencies error.missing-import error.unknown-namespace
docs: IMPORTS.md METHOD-SUGAR.md STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md

== WHAT IT IS
    import std::list            this file depends on namespace std::list
    import std::abi::bytes      a nested namespace (stdlib/abi/bytes.bot)
    import type std::byte::Byte one type's short name: Byte
    import ::std::str           a rooted path: the same as import std::str
    import game                 the application module game.bot

After `import std::list`, `std::list::length(xs)` (or, rooted,
`::std::list::length(xs)`) is allowed and the namespace's functions are
candidates for method syntax (`xs.length()`). An import injects no shorter
name: `length(xs)` is still unbound, and `list::length(xs)` would name an
application namespace list, never std::list.

== RULES
  * Imports come first in the file (after an optional `direct module`
    line); an import elsewhere is a syntax error.
  * Exact: `import std::abi` does not import `std::abi::bytes`, and the
    reverse.
  * Not transitive: if A imports B and B imports C, A writes `import C` to
    use C's names, methods or types.
  * Using `NS::x` without `import NS` is MISSING-IMPORT, which names the
    line to add.
  * Every path is resolved from the root; a leading `::` says so
    explicitly and changes nothing else (concept.standard-library).
  * No aliases, wildcards, `from ... import` or re-exports.
  * `import type` imports a `type` declaration (integer domain,
    refinement) or an enum by its short name, not a struct
    (capability.struct-type-import).
  * The entry program gets no implicit imports; the root primitives (+,
    ==, mod, hash, argv, ...) need none.

== WHY
A file's header lists everything it depends on, so a reader (and the
compiler) knows which names and methods are in play without following
other files; nothing a dependency imports leaks into the file.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str
import type std::byte::Byte

fn low(b: Byte) -> int:
    b

[std::str::length("abc"), "abc".length(), low(7)]
@caption A qualified call, a method call, a short type name
@value [3, 3, 7]
@end

@example reject
std::list::length([1, 2])
@caption A namespace must be imported before it is named
@error SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT
@at 1:1
@end

== MORE
IMPORTS.md; concept.modules for where a namespace's file is found.

@topic concept.modules
title: Modules: one file, one namespace
summary: A module is a source file loaded by `import`, its namespace its path; it holds declarations and immutable values, and a program's own modules are the files beside its entry file.
index: concepts
alias: module; modules; namespace; module binding; application module; global constant
keywords: module, namespace, file, stdlib, application root, module binding, MODULE-CONTEXT, MODULE-IMMUTABLE, RESERVED-NAMESPACE, SELF-IMPORT, global, constant
related: concept.imports concept.standard-library concept.opaque-structs concept.direct-modules concept.contexts rule.module-bindings rule.application-modules rule.package-modules principle.explicit-dependencies error.module-context error.module-collision error.unknown-namespace
docs: IMPORTS.md MODULE-BINDINGS.md NATIVE-MODULES.md POST-2048-PRODUCT-FIXES.md BOT-MODULES-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
One file is one namespace: `stdlib/abi/bytes.bot` is `std::abi::bytes` (the
standard library's directory is the namespace std), a file `game.bot` beside
the entry file `main.bot` is the application module `game`. There is no
`namespace` declaration; the entry program is in no namespace. A module
contains function, type, struct, enum, error, trait and context declarations
and immutable value bindings, each named outside as `game::member`.

== RULES
  * Resolution of `import NAME` (rule.application-modules): `std::NAME`
    is stdlib/NAME.bot beside the compiler, any other NAME is `NAME.bot`
    in the entry file's directory (the application root); nothing else
    (no working directory, path variable, parent or fallback). A local
    `list.bot` and `std::list` are two modules; an application file
    under std/ is RESERVED-NAMESPACE; importing the entry file is
    SELF-IMPORT.
  * Installed packages (rule.package-modules): a namespace whose first
    segment names a package in the importing file's scope
    (.bot_modules/NAME) is that package's module, and each installed copy
    is its own instance, with its own types and errors.
  * A module has no top-level statements other than bindings and
    declarations. A value binding runs once, before the entry program,
    and must be context-free (MODULE-CONTEXT) and transitively immutable
    (MODULE-IMMUTABLE): Ints, Strings, Bools, unit and Lists of them are
    accepted; a struct or a MutableArray is not -- write a function that
    returns it instead.
  * There are no mutable globals: state that the whole program shares is
    a context (concept.contexts).
  * Import cycles are rejected (CYCLE).

== WHY
Modules are compile-time namespaces: a module value is resolved to one
binding before the program runs, with no namespace object or run-time
lookup. Immutable, context-free module values can be computed once at
startup without any hidden initialization order or shared mutable state.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

std::list::length([1, 2, 3])
@caption A standard module's member, qualified
@value 3
@end

@example reject
import geometry

0
@caption No standard module and no application file of that name
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
@at 1:8
@end

== MORE
IMPORTS.md ("Application modules"); MODULE-BINDINGS.md.

@topic concept.standard-library
title: The standard library: the namespace std, and rooted names
summary: Every standard namespace is under std (std::list, std::str, std::linux::io), read from stdlib/ beside the compiler; a leading :: roots any namespace path explicitly.
index: concepts
alias: std; standard library; stdlib; ::std; absolute namespace; rooted name; root namespace; where is the standard library
keywords: std, stdlib, standard library, ::, rooted, absolute, root, namespace, reserved, local module, list.bot
related: concept.imports concept.modules rule.application-modules error.reserved-namespace error.rooted-name error.unknown-namespace
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md IMPORTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
The standard library is one namespace tree with the compiler-owned root
`std`: `std::list`, `std::str`, `std::abi::bytes`, `std::linux::io`, ...
Its source is the directory stdlib/ that ships with the compiler (the
checkout's, or the one packed in the single executable), so it never
depends on the working directory, the application or the environment, and
a compiled executable needs no source beside it. Some standard namespaces
are compiler intrinsics only (`std::str`, `std::char`).

    import std::list                   the standard list
    import ::std::list                 the same, rooted explicitly
    import list                        the application's own list.bot

== RULES
  * Each standard symbol has one name: `std::list::at` (or its method
    spelling, `xs.at(i)`); there is no flat `list::at`, no alias and no
    implicit import.
  * `std` is reserved: no application file is ever a standard namespace,
    and std itself is no namespace (`import std` names nothing).
  * A leading `::` roots a path: every path is resolved from the root
    already, so `::std::list` and `std::list` are one namespace and one
    identity; a rooted reference names a namespace member
    (`::std::list::at`, `::game::Color::Red`), never a bare name.
  * `std::` says who owns a namespace, not where it runs: std::linux::*
    is Linux's (UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM elsewhere).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import ::std::str
import std::list

[::std::str::length("abc"), std::list::length([1])]
@caption Rooted and unrooted names of standard namespaces
@value [3, 1]
@end

@example reject
import std::list

list::length([1])
@caption The standard list is spelled std::list
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
@at 3:1
@end

