STD-NAMESPACE.md; `-help stdlib` lists the standard namespaces.

@topic concept.if-expressions
title: `if`, `elif`, `else` and `if` as a value
summary: `if c:` / `elif c2:` / `else:` chooses a branch; an `if` is a value only as the whole right side of `=` or the value of `return` -- never an operand or argument, so bind it to a name first.
index: concepts
alias: if statement; elif; else branch; if expression; conditional expression; ternary
keywords: if, elif, else, condition, branch, value, ternary, operand, argument, placement, NOT-BOOLEAN
related: concept.booleans concept.loops concept.unit rule.if-placement principle.guard-clauses principle.bind-before-use error.not-boolean error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md ELIF.md

== WHAT IT IS
    sign = if n < 0:
        -1
    elif n == 0:
        0
    else:
        1

Conditions are tested in order, each at most once; only the selected
branch runs, and its last expression is the `if`'s value. `elif` is
exactly `else:` holding one nested `if`. Without `else`, the value is unit
when no branch is taken. Each branch is a scope of its own.

== RULES
  * Placement: an `if` is a value only as the whole right side of `=` or
    as the value of `return` (and, like any statement, as the last
    expression of a body). `1 + if c: ...` and `f(if c: ...)` are syntax
    errors ("an "if" value must be the whole right side of "=" or
    "return""): bind the `if` to a name, then use the name.
  * There is no one-line form (`if c: a else: b`) and no ternary operator.
  * A condition must be a Bool (NOT-BOOLEAN when it is evaluated).
  * Branches may end in `return`, `fail`, `break` or `continue`.

== WHY
An `if`'s branches are indented blocks on their own lines, so it cannot
sit inside an expression; binding the result to a name gives the value a
name and keeps each line one step (principle.bind-before-use).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn sign(n: int) -> int:
    if n < 0:
        -1
    elif n == 0:
        0
    else:
        1

label = if sign(-4) < 0:
    "negative"
else:
    "non-negative"
label
@caption An if chain as a function's value; an if bound to a name
@value "negative"
@end

@example reject
n = 3
x = 1 + if n > 0:
    1
else:
    2
x
@caption An if cannot be an operand: bind it first
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:9
@end

