@topic concept.loops
title: Loops: `loop:`, collecting loops, `break` and `continue`
summary: `loop x in xs:` and `loop i from a to b:` collect their body's values into a List; a bare `loop:` repeats until `break`; a loop value is never an operand or argument.
index: concepts
alias: loop; for loop; while loop; collecting loop; break and continue; counted loop
keywords: loop, in, from, to, through, down from, break, continue, while, for, collect, map, filter, iteration, iterator, iterate, range, placement
related: concept.lockstep concept.lists concept.if-expressions concept.mutable-map concept.mutable-set rule.loop-placement rule.control-placement capability.map-filter proof.list-bounds task.iterate-with-index
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md

== WHAT IT IS
    loop x in xs:                  every element of a List (or vector), or
                                   of a MutableMap or MutableSet in
                                   insertion order
    loop i from a to b:            a <= i < b, ascending
    loop i from a through b:       a <= i <= b
    loop i down from b to a:       b >= i > a, descending
    loop i down from b through a:  b >= i >= a
    loop:                          repeat until break or return

A loop with a clause collects: its value is the List of the body's last
expressions, one per iteration. `continue` skips an iteration (contributes
nothing), `break` ends the loop with the List so far; a bare `loop:` ended
by `break` has the value unit. Bounds are evaluated once; a numeric loop
whose bounds are empty runs zero times and never reverses direction.

== RULES
  * Placement: a collecting loop's value is used as the whole right side of
    `=` or after `return`, or as a function body's last expression; a loop
    is never an operand or a call argument ("expected an expression, found
    "loop""). Bind it to a name first.
  * `break` takes no value; `break` and `continue` are legal only inside a
    loop of the same function.
  * There is no `while` or `for`: `loop:` with an `if ...: break` is the
    while loop, and a collecting loop with `continue` is map and filter.
  * A bare loop cannot accumulate (no assignment): use a collecting loop,
    recursion, or a MutableVector.

== WHY
Collecting loops replace map, filter and list-building variables with one
construct whose result is a value, so no mutation is needed to build a
List; loop counters and elements are fresh bindings in each iteration.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
squares = loop i from 1 through 4:
    i * i
evens = loop x in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]:
    if x.mod(2) == 1:
        continue
    if x > 4:
        break
    x
[squares, evens]
@caption Mapping, filtering and stopping early
@value [[1, 4, 9, 16], [2, 4]]
@end

@example reject
import std::list

n = std::list::length(loop i from 0 to 3:
    i)
n
@caption A loop cannot be a call argument: bind it first
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 3:23
@end

== MORE
COLLECTING-LOOPS.md, RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md; concept.lockstep for
iterating several domains together.

@topic concept.lockstep
title: Lockstep loops: several domains with a proven common count
summary: `loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():` advances several clauses together, one body per common position; every clause must have a statically provable equal iteration count.
index: concepts
alias: lockstep loop; zip lists; parallel iteration
keywords: lockstep, and, zip, enumerate, index, LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN, LOCKSTEP-UNEQUAL, cardinality, equal length
related: concept.loops proof.lockstep proof.list-bounds task.iterate-with-index error.lockstep-unproven error.lockstep-unequal
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md

== WHAT IT IS
Loop clauses joined with `and` run in lockstep: one body execution per
common position, every clause advancing together (`continue` advances
all, `break` leaves the loop). The usual use is an element with its index.

== RULES
  * Every clause's iteration count must be proven equal to the first's at
    compile time: LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN when the compiler cannot prove it,
    LOCKSTEP-UNEQUAL when the counts provably differ. There is no
    shortest-wins rule and no run-time length check.
  * Proven: a List and a counted clause over its own length
    (`i from 0 to xs.length()`, or `down from xs.length() - 1 through 0`);
    numeric clauses whose bounds are the same linear form; the same List
    or an alias; equal-length literals; a List and a loop collected from it
    without `continue`/`break`.
  * Not proven: two List parameters (even after an equality guard), lists
    from separate calls, a filtered loop's output.

== WHY
Lockstep is not `zip`: a silent truncation to the shorter domain hides
bugs, and a run-time check would turn them into crashes. Requiring a proof
makes "one position per element" a compile-time fact.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn numbered(names: List[str]) -> List[str]:
    loop name in names and i from 0 to names.length():
        if i == 0:
            continue
        name

numbered(["zero", "one", "two"])
@caption Elements with their index
@value ["one", "two"]
@end

@example reject
fn sums(xs: List[int], ys: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and y in ys:
        x + y

0
@caption Two unrelated lists have no proven common length
@error CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN
@at 2:5
@end

