@topic concept.errors
title: Declared errors: `error`, `errors`, `fail` and `on` handlers
summary: A function lists its declared errors (`errors E`) and raises one with `fail E`; each call handles each possible error (`on E:`), admits it in its errors clause, or is proven not to raise it.
index: concepts
alias: declared errors; error handling; on handler; fail statement; errors clause; error propagation
keywords: error, errors, fail, on, handler, propagate, raise, exception, catch, try, throw, UNHANDLED-ERROR, KNOWN-ERROR, IndexNotFound, UNDECLARED-ERROR
related: concept.error-payloads concept.known-errors concept.functions concept.function-types rule.unhandled-errors rule.one-handler-per-error rule.known-error proof.completions principle.errors-over-sentinels capability.exceptions capability.multi-error-handler task.handle-several-errors error.unhandled-error error.undeclared-error
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    error Negative                         a declaration (top level)
    fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
        if n < 0:
            fail Negative                  completes the call with Negative
        n

    x = checked(n):                        a handled call
        on Negative:
            0                              the call's value when it fails

A call may be followed by `:` and one handler per error. When the call
fails with E, the `on E:` block runs and its last expression becomes the
value of the call expression; `return` in a handler leaves the enclosing
function instead. Builtin errors (IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun,
UpperOverrun, InvalidArgumentEncoding, ...) need no declaration.

== RULES
  * Every error a call may produce must be handled at the call, admitted
    by the enclosing function's `errors` clause (then it propagates to
    the caller), or proven impossible (UNHANDLED-ERROR otherwise). The
    program's top level has no errors clause: handle there.
  * A handler names exactly one error: `on A, B:` is a syntax error;
    write two handlers. There is no catch-all.
  * A call proven to always fail is KNOWN-ERROR, even with a handler
    (concept.known-errors).
  * Handlers attach to a call (or method call), as a statement, the right
    side of `=`, or after `return`; they are not a block around several
    statements.
  * `fail` and `errors` name declared errors only (UNDECLARED-ERROR); an
    error of module m is `m::Name` outside m.

== WHY
Errors are part of a function's contract, checked by the compiler, not
exceptions that may surface anywhere: every failure a caller can see is
named in a signature, and every call site says what happens when it
occurs. Provable success needs no handler, so checks do not pile up.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
error Negative
error TooBig

fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative, TooBig:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    if n > 100:
        fail TooBig
    n

fn doubled(n: int) -> int errors Negative, TooBig:
    checked(n) * 2

fn clamp(n: int) -> int:
    doubled(n):
        on Negative:
            0
        on TooBig:
            return 200

[clamp(-3), clamp(500), clamp(7)]
@caption Propagation by `errors`; one handler per error; a handler's value
@value [0, 200, 14]
@end

@example reject
error Negative

fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn doubled(n: int) -> int:
    checked(n) * 2

0
@caption Neither handled nor admitted by doubled's errors clause
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 9:5
@end

