EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md; STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md;
task.handle-several-errors.

@topic concept.error-payloads
title: Error payloads: errors with named fields
summary: An error may declare named fields (`error NotFound:` then `uri: str`); `fail NotFound {uri: u}` supplies every field, and a handler receives them with `on NotFound details:` or `on NotFound {uri}:`.
index: concepts
alias: error payload; error fields; error with data; error details
keywords: payload, error, field, fail, on, details, destructure, MISSING-FIELD, module-qualified, identity
related: concept.errors concept.destructuring concept.structs concept.enums capability.multi-error-handler error.missing-field
docs: ERROR-PAYLOADS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    error PageNotFound:
        uri: str
        status: int

    fail PageNotFound {uri: u, status: 404}

A handler receives the payload in one of three ways:

    on PageNotFound:                   ignored
    on PageNotFound details:           bound whole (details.uri)
    on PageNotFound {status}:          destructured (concept.destructuring)

== RULES
  * `fail` gives every declared field exactly once, each proven
    admissible, like a struct construction (MISSING-FIELD, UNKNOWN-FIELD).
  * `on` selects by the error's identity only: no value patterns, guards
    or payload-driven selection. Two errors with the same fields are
    unrelated.
  * `errors` clauses and Fn{...} types name identities, never payloads.
  * An error's identity is module-qualified: module m's `error NotFound`
    is `m::NotFound` outside m.
  * A payload-free error stays a plain name; payloads cost nothing for
    errors without fields.

== WHY
A failure often needs to say more than its name (which URI, which status)
without inventing a sentinel return value; the payload is a statically
typed struct, so handlers read it like any value.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
error PageNotFound:
    uri: str
    status: int

fn fetch(uri: str) -> str errors PageNotFound:
    if uri == "/missing":
        fail PageNotFound {uri: uri, status: 404}
    "page"

fn status_of(uri: str) -> int:
    fetch(uri):
        on PageNotFound {status}:
            return status
    200

[status_of("/missing"), status_of("/home")]
@caption A destructured payload in the handler
@value [404, 200]
@end

@example reject
error PageNotFound:
    uri: str
    status: int

fn fetch(uri: str) -> str errors PageNotFound:
    fail PageNotFound {uri: uri}

0
@caption Every payload field must be given
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD
@at 6:23
@end

