@topic concept.known-errors
title: Statically known outcomes: proven success and proven failure
summary: The compiler decides some calls' outcomes: a call proven to succeed needs no handler, and a call proven to always fail is KNOWN-ERROR -- rejected even when a handler would catch the error.
index: concepts
alias: proven call outcome; call that cannot fail; call proven to fail
keywords: KNOWN-ERROR, always fails, handler, proven, completion, literal, guard, impossible error, no handler needed, test
related: concept.errors concept.negative-tests rule.known-error rule.unhandled-errors proof.completions proof.list-bounds proof.ranges error.known-error
docs: STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md

== WHAT IT IS
The completion analysis computes, for each call, which declared errors it
can produce under the facts proven for its arguments there: literal
values, ranges from guards, List and String lengths, and the callee's own
`fail` sites and their conditions.

  * Proven success: an error that cannot occur needs no handler and no
    errors clause (`[1, 2, 3].at(1)`; `digit(7)` for a converter that fails
    only outside 0..9).
  * Proven failure: a call that can never complete normally is the
    KNOWN-ERROR diagnostic ("this call can never complete normally under
    the facts proven for its arguments here -- it always produces the
    declared error(s) ...; a handler does not make a statically known
    failure legal").

== RULES
  * KNOWN-ERROR applies with or without a handler, at the top level and in
    functions, including nested functions written as tests.
  * Only what the analysis proves counts: a failure that depends on facts
    it does not track (for instance String equality) is not decided, and
    the call is treated as "may fail".

== WHY
A call that always fails is dead code wearing a handler: the handler is
the real behavior, and writing it directly is clearer. Rejecting it keeps
error handling for outcomes that depend on the input.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

xs = [10, 20, 30]
xs.at(1)
@caption Proven success: no handler needed
@value 20
@end

@example reject
import std::list

x = [1, 2, 3].at(5):
    on IndexNotFound:
        0
x
@caption Proven failure is rejected even though it is handled
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 3:5
@end

== MORE
To test a failing call on purpose, see concept.negative-tests.
