@topic concept.contexts
title: Contexts: execution-environment values without globals
summary: A `context struct` value installed with `with context EXPR` reaches every function declaring `context name: T`; callers pass nothing, and the compiler proves the installation precedes the call.
index: concepts
alias: context struct; with context; context parameter; dependency injection; environment value
keywords: context, with, installed, requirement, MISSING-CONTEXT, DUPLICATE-CONTEXT, CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE, global, singleton, LinuxIO, suite
related: concept.context-traits concept.context-owned-state concept.opaque-structs concept.direct-modules concept.testing proof.contexts rule.context-installation error.missing-context error.duplicate-context error.context-function-value error.context-installation-unsupported
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md CONTEXT-OWNED-AFFINE-STATE-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
    context struct Config:
        verbose: bool

    fn verbose?(context config: Config) -> bool:
        config.verbose

    with context Config {verbose: true}
    verbose?()

The context section comes last in the parameter list; `config` is an
ordinary local inside the function, and callers do not pass it (it does
not count in the arity). Only the function that uses a context declares
it: every caller's requirement is derived from the call graph.

== RULES
  * `with context EXPR` is a statement of the entry program's top level
    (CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED elsewhere, for now); EXPR's type must
    be exactly one context struct.
  * A context is identified by its type: at most one installed value per
    type (DUPLICATE-CONTEXT).
  * Every top-level call must come after the installation of everything it
    transitively needs (MISSING-CONTEXT, with the call chain).
  * A function needing a context can be called or aliased but not passed
    or stored as a value (CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE).
  * The context value is immutable; a MutableVector field inside it is a
    place the functions may mutate (stdlib/test.bot's suite does this).
  * The installed context owns what it holds, affine members included (C
    pointers, coroutines, vectors of them): a function reaches them as
    places, never as copies (concept.context-owned-state).

== WHY
Contexts replace global variables and hidden singletons: dependencies on
the environment (I/O, a clock, a test suite) are visible in the functions
that use them, proven at compile time, and installed exactly once. Natively
a context lives in a fixed data slot: no hidden argument and no lookup.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

context struct Config:
    verbose: bool
    name: str

fn greeting(context config: Config) -> str:
    if config.verbose:
        return "hello, ".concat(config.name)
    "hi"

fn main_text() -> str:
    greeting()

with context Config {verbose: true, name: "Ada"}
main_text()
@caption main_text needs Config through greeting; nobody passes it
@value "hello, Ada"
@end

@example reject
context struct Config:
    verbose: bool

fn verbose?(context config: Config) -> bool:
    config.verbose

x = verbose?()
with context Config {verbose: true}
x
@caption The call runs before the installation
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT
@at 7:5
@end

