@topic concept.context-owned-state
title: Context-owned state: an installed context owns its affine members
summary: The installed context owns its affine members; a function requiring it reaches them as places, observed where they are stored and moved only by a collection's take, pop or swap.
index: concepts
alias: context holding a pointer; context owns a resource; airlock; acquire a resource after installation; element place
keywords: context, affine, owned, place, element, observe, pointer, resource, open, close, airlock, push, pop, take, swap
related: concept.contexts concept.affine concept.mutable-vector error.affine-context-copy-out stdlib.std::mutable_vector::element stdlib.std::mutable_array::element concept.private concept.opaque-structs
docs: CONTEXT-OWNED-AFFINE-STATE-MILESTONE-1.md CONTEXTS.md AFFINE-VALUES.md

== WHAT IT IS
Three things are distinct:

  * a *context requirement* -- `fn draw(context sdl: Sdl)` -- says what
    environment a function needs;
  * the *installation* -- `with context sdl::create()` in the entry
    program -- moves a value into the installed context, which owns its
    state for the rest of the run;
  * *context-member place access*: inside a function requiring the
    context, `sdl`, `sdl.renderer` and `sdl.sessions.element(0).window`
    are places of that installed state, not copies of it.

An affine value at such a place (a C pointer, a coroutine, a struct or
vector holding one) is observed where it is stored -- a C call, `null?`,
`clone`'s receiver, a field read -- and never copied out
(AFFINE-CONTEXT-COPY-OUT). A collection member is mutated in place: push
moves a value into the context, take, pop and swap move one out again,
leaving no stale copy behind. `std::mutable_vector::element(v, i)` (and
`std::mutable_array::element`) is the place of an element the context
owns, for observing it where it is.

== RULES
  * Installation consumes its value (a later use of the binding is
    USE-AFTER-MOVE); the installed context is never released: contexts
    live as long as the program, and affinity is at most once -- release
    what you acquired with an explicit operation (a `close`).
  * A direct member is owned for the program's lifetime: it can be
    observed and cloned explicitly, not moved out. State acquired and
    released after installation lives in a collection member.
  * An element place is no value: it is used where it is written, fails
    with IndexNotFound when the element is missing, and a function using one
    declares that error for its caller to handle.
  * The whole context is never copied while it holds affine state: no
    binding, argument, result or destructuring of it, and no closure
    capturing it (a nested function declares the context itself).
  * Unrestricted contexts are unchanged: copied, passed and compared freely.

== WHY
A context is an authority boundary: the module that declares it can keep
the raw resources it protects -- a library's handles -- inside it, behind
ordinary value-taking operations, while affine tracking still guarantees
that nothing duplicates them implicitly. It is not a destructor or a borrow
scope: nothing is released when the program ends, and no reference to a
member ever leaves the expression that reaches it.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c
import std::mutable_vector

struct Session:
    window: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]
    id: int

opaque context struct Video:
    sessions: MutableVector[Session]

fn open(id: int, context v: Video):
    v.sessions.push(Session {window: std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void](), id: id})

fn window_null?(context v: Video) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    v.sessions.element(0).window.null?()

fn opened?() -> bool:
    seen = window_null?():
        on IndexNotFound:
            return false
    seen

fn close(context v: Video) -> int:
    s = v.sessions.pop():
        on IndexNotFound:
            return 0
    {window, id} = s
    id

with context Video {sessions: std::mutable_vector::from_list([])}
before = opened?()
open(7)
during = opened?()
closed = close()
[before, during, closed, opened?()]
@caption Installed closed, acquired by push, observed in place, released by pop
@value [false, true, 7, false]
@end

@example reject
import std::ffi::c

opaque context struct Device:
    root: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]

fn root(context d: Device) -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]:
    d.root

with context Device {root: std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()}
0
@caption Returning the context's pointer would give the caller a second owner
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CONTEXT-COPY-OUT
@at 7:5
@end

