@topic concept.context-traits
title: Context traits: environments named by their operations (std::io::IO)
summary: A `context trait` lists operations an installed context must provide; a function requiring it (`context io: std::io::IO`) works with whichever installed context implements it, selected statically.
index: concepts
alias: context trait; portable io; std::io::IO trait
keywords: context trait, io, IO, print_line, LinuxIO, portable, implementation, selection, AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION, MISSING-CONTEXT, write_text
related: concept.contexts concept.traits task.print-output backend.io stdlib.module.std::io error.missing-context error.ambiguous-context-implementation
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md CONTEXTS.md TRAITS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    context trait Clock:
        fn now() -> int

A context struct implements it structurally: its own module declares a
function of each operation's name taking the context (`fn now(context clock:
FixedClock) -> int`). A function declaring `context clock: Clock` calls
`clock.now()`; the compiler selects the one installed context implementing
Clock and compiles the call as a direct call of that implementation.
stdlib/io.bot's `std::io::IO` is the standard one: `std::io::print_line`
works with `std::linux::io::create()` installed.

== RULES
  * Exactly one installed context may implement the trait at a call
    (none: MISSING-CONTEXT; several: AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION).
    It is never ranked or chosen at run time.
  * Implementations are read only from the concrete context's own
    namespace, never from the caller's.
  * A context trait has no value: it is not a type for bindings, fields or
    Lists.
  * Printing through io runs on the native backends only: the Linux
    implementation needs machine addresses (NATIVE-ONLY on interp and
    compile; backend.io).

== WHY
Code written against `std::io::IO` is portable: the entry program decides
the environment once, and every function needing it compiles to direct
calls, with no dispatch, hidden argument or provider table at run time.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
context trait Clock:
    fn now() -> int

context struct FixedClock:
    time: int

fn now(context clock: FixedClock) -> int:
    clock.time

fn elapsed(start: int, context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.now() - start

with context FixedClock {time: 42}
elapsed(40)
@caption FixedClock implements Clock through its module's `now`
@value 2
@end

@example accept
import std::io
import std::linux::io

fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
    std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

with context std::linux::io::create()
hello():
    on std::io::WriteFailed:
        unit
0
@caption Portable output through std::io::IO (native backends)
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@output Hello, world!
@value 0
@end

