@topic concept.traits
title: Traits: static, structural views of values
summary: A `trait` declares operations; a trait-typed parameter accepts any value whose own module provides them, uses only those, and is compiled away by monomorphization.
index: concepts
alias: trait; traits; interface; type class; protocol
keywords: trait, interface, structural, conformance, view, witness, monomorphization, TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED, TRAIT-VIEW-MISUSE, polymorphism, dispatch, vtable
related: concept.context-traits concept.hashable concept.specialization concept.method-sugar error.trait-not-satisfied error.trait-view-misuse
docs: TRAITS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    trait Named:
        fn name(value: Named) -> str

A concrete type satisfies Named when the namespace that owns it declares a
function `name` whose signature fits the requirement with the trait
replaced by the type. There is no `implements` declaration. A value enters
the trait at a trait-typed parameter or result; inside, it is a trait view
on which only the trait's operations are allowed (as method calls).

== RULES
  * Conformance reads only the owner namespace of the concrete type:
    nothing the caller imports or declares takes part
    (TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED lists what is missing).
  * Through a view, a field, a concrete function or a type test is
    TRAIT-VIEW-MISUSE.
  * Every trait-polymorphic function becomes one ordinary function per
    concrete type its calls pass; a trait operation becomes a direct call.
    Nothing of the trait exists at run time.
  * A trait is declared at top level and needs at least one requirement;
    requirements are signatures only.
  * The built-in trait Hashable (concept.hashable) is used the same way,
    but its witnesses come from the language, not from a module's
    functions.

== WHY
Traits abstract over values without a run-time cost or a second dispatch
mechanism: the trait view limits what the source may assume, and the
compiler keeps the concrete type to specialize every call.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

describe(Person {value: "Alice"})
@caption describe is compiled for Person; name is a direct call
@value "Alice"
@end

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Pet:
    species: str

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

describe(Pet {species: "cat"})
@caption Pet's module declares no `name` for Pet
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
@at 10:10
@end

