@topic concept.hashable
title: Hashable: values with total equality and total hashing
summary: Hashable is a built-in trait, used like any trait, whose witnesses the language supplies: exactly the types whose every value has total equality and a hash.
index: concepts
alias: Hashable; hashable; hashable trait; hash key; map key; set key; dictionary key
keywords: Hashable, hash, ==, equality, total, witness, built-in trait, callable, function, key, collision, keyed hash, TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
related: concept.traits stdlib.hash stdlib.== error.trait-not-satisfied error.equality error.internal-namespace
docs: HASHABLE-AND-KEYED-HASH.md TRAITS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn fingerprint(x: Hashable) -> int:
        hash(x)

Hashable is a trait like any other to the code that uses it: a parameter,
a result or a function of it is monomorphized per concrete type, and
`x.hash()` is its one operation (the public hash). What differs is who
supplies the witnesses: no module implements Hashable, and no source
declaration can make a type satisfy it. A type is Hashable exactly when
every value of it has total equality (== with it never stops with
EQUALITY) and a hash, and so equal values always hash equally:

  * Int and every integer domain, String, Bool, unit, UnicodeChar,
    Float64, enum cases;
  * a List or ImmutableSet of Hashable elements;
  * a struct (also an opaque one) whose every field is Hashable.

Not Hashable: a function (functions have no equality), a coroutine handle,
a MutableArray or MutableVector, a C pointer or native view, a
DecimalRounded value, and any struct, List or set that holds one; `any`
and an untyped field, whose values are not known (one may hold a
function); a view of another trait; an imprint variable -- unless a
constrained slot of its signature holds it (`fn f(m: Map[K, V])` with
`struct Map[K satisfies Hashable, V]`: concept.parameterized-structs),
then it is.

== RULES
  * A value enters Hashable at a Hashable parameter or result, like any
    trait: a type that is not Hashable is TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED there, with
    the field or element that is not, never later.
  * hash(x) and x == y never stop with EQUALITY when the operands are
    Hashable; the compiler drops the failure path.
  * hash(x) of a value statically known not to be Hashable (a function,
    a struct with a function field) is TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED at compile time.
    hash of a value of unknown type (an untyped parameter) is still
    checked when it runs (EQUALITY), as before.
  * Hashable cannot be declared, implemented or named by a type, struct,
    enum, layout or trait of a program: the name is reserved.

== WHY
Hash-based collections need keys whose equality and hash agree and never
fail; a function's equality is not total, so a function is no key. Making
that a trait lets a function demand it of its arguments statically. The
standard library's hash structures place keys with a separate,
collision-hardened hash: SipHash-1-3 of the key under a secret the runtime
draws once per process, which no program can read, pass or print. Unlike
the public hash, its numbers differ from run to run, so keys chosen to
collide under the public hash do not collide there; no program observes
them.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

fn fingerprint(x: Hashable) -> int:
    hash(x)

[fingerprint(Point {x: 1, y: 2}) == hash(Point {x: 1, y: 2}), fingerprint("apple") == "apple".hash()]
@caption fingerprint is compiled for Point and for String; both hashes are total
@value [true, true]
@end

@example reject
struct Handler:
    name: str
    run: Fn{args: [int], return: int}

fn fingerprint(x: Hashable) -> int:
    hash(x)

fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

fingerprint(Handler {name: "double", run: twice})
@caption Handler's field run is a function, which has no total equality
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
@at 11:13
@end

