@topic concept.affine
title: Affine values: one owner, moved, released
summary: A coroutine handle, and any struct, List or vector that owns one, is affine: it has one usable owner, moves through bindings, arguments, results and fields, and is released where its owner dies.
index: concepts
alias: affine; affine value; ownership; move semantics; use after move
keywords: affine, move, owner, release, drop, USE-AFTER-MOVE, coroutine handle, copy, duplicate, unrestricted, AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED
related: concept.coroutines concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array concept.function-types proof.ownership rule.affine-use error.use-after-move
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md

== WHAT IT IS
Most values are unrestricted: they can be copied and used any number of
times. An affine value may have only one usable owner at a time. The
coroutine handle is the one primitive affine value; a struct, anonymous
struct, List, MutableVector or MutableArray that owns one is affine too,
by its type. `other = step` moves the handle: `step` is dead afterwards.

== RULES
  * A use after a move is USE-AFTER-MOVE, naming where the value moved.
  * Moves happen by binding, passing as an argument, returning, storing
    in a struct field or a List element. An affine field is taken out by
    destructuring the struct.
  * An affine value may be dropped unused; it is released (a suspended
    coroutine's stack freed) right after its owner's last use, or when a
    return, fail, break, continue or error leaves its scope first.
  * Not supported: capturing one in a closure
    (AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED) and `==` on it
    (AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED); a second use such as `[step, step]` is
    USE-AFTER-MOVE.
  * Ownership is entirely static: no reference count or moved flag exists
    at run time.

== WHY
A coroutine is one evolving execution: copying the handle would let two
owners resume the same continuation. Making ownership a property of the
type lets handles be stored in structs and queues while the compiler
still proves that each one is used by one owner and cleaned up.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn ticks() -> int:
    yield 1
    yield 2
    3

struct Job:
    name: str
    step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}

fn start(name: str) -> Job:
    coroutine {step} = ticks()
    Job {name: name, step: step}

fn advance(job: Job) -> int:
    {step} = job
    step()

advance(start("job-a"))
@caption A struct owning a handle is affine and moves whole
@value 2
@end

@example reject
fn ticks() -> int:
    yield 1
    2

coroutine {step} = ticks()
a = step
b = step
0
@caption step moved into a
@error CORE SEMANTIC USE-AFTER-MOVE
@at 7:5
@end

