@topic concept.coroutines
title: Coroutines: `yield`, eager start, resume
summary: `coroutine {step, first} = f(x)` starts f(x) and runs it to its first `yield`; `step(message)` resumes it to the next; the handle is affine.
index: concepts
alias: coroutines; yield; generator; resume a coroutine
keywords: coroutine, yield, resume, step, first, done?, handle, generator, iterator, UNWRAPPED-YIELD, suspend, message, scheduler
related: concept.affine concept.function-types concept.mutable-vector concept.testing stdlib.module.std::coroutine error.unwrapped-yield error.use-after-move
docs: COROUTINES.md AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINE-FUSION-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
`coroutine {step, first} = counter(3)` starts the call: `first` is what it
yields first, `step` the handle (either field may be omitted or renamed).
`yield v` sends v outward and suspends the whole coroutine, however deep in
its call stack; it evaluates to the message of the next resume. A
function that resumes with messages declares their struct type last in
its parameter list: `fn worker(seed: int, resume Command) -> Event:`, and
is resumed as `step(Command {...})`. `step()` resumes the zero-message
protocol.

== RULES
  * Start is eager: the construction runs the call to its first yield,
    its return or an unhandled fail.
  * A function that may yield is called only inside a coroutine (or
    started as one): UNWRAPPED-YIELD otherwise; it cannot become a
    function value.
  * After completion the handle keeps returning the final result; a failed
    coroutine raises its error again. `std::coroutine::done?(step)` (`import
    coroutine`) is true once it has done either.
  * The handle is affine (concept.affine): moved, never copied, released
    after its last use.
  * `yield` is a keyword with the lowest precedence: `1 + (yield x)`.

== WHY
Coroutines give schedulers, generators and test runners resumable
computations without threads or callbacks; affine handles make "who may
resume this" a compile-time fact. Native coroutines switch stacks on Linux
x86-64 and AArch64 and macOS AArch64, except a coroutine whose handle never
leaves the function that constructs it (and whose yielding helpers are
neither recursive nor closures): the native compiler builds that one into
the function itself -- no stack of its own, no switch, its suspended state
a few registers -- with the same meaning (COROUTINE-FUSION-MILESTONE-1.md).
The Tcl backends run coroutines everywhere.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::coroutine

fn counter(limit: int) -> int:
    loop i from 0 to limit:
        yield i
    -1

coroutine {step, first} = counter(3)
a = step()
b = step()
c = step()
d = step()
[first, a, b, c, d, std::coroutine::done?(step)]
@caption Yields 0, 1, 2, then the final result, repeated
@value [0, 1, 2, -1, -1, true]
@end

@example accept
struct Add:
    amount: int

fn summer(start: int, resume Add) -> int:
    m1 = yield start
    m2 = yield start + m1.amount
    start + m1.amount + m2.amount

coroutine {step, first} = summer(10)
a = step(Add {amount: 5})
b = step(Add {amount: 7})
[first, a, b]
@caption Resumed with messages of a declared struct type
@value [10, 15, 22]
@end

@example reject
fn gen() -> int:
    yield 1
    2

x = gen()
x
@caption A yielding function called outside a coroutine
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNWRAPPED-YIELD
@at 5:5
@end

