@topic concept.mutable-vector
title: MutableVector[T]: a growable mutable value
summary: MutableVector[T] grows and shrinks in place (push, pop, take, swap); it is a value that assignment copies, and every mutation must name a place of the mutating function.
index: concepts
alias: MutableVector; growable list; dynamic array; vector type
keywords: mutable_vector, push, pop, take, swap, clear, at, length, place, copy-on-write, MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER, MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE, affine
related: concept.mutable-array concept.values concept.affine concept.lists concept.place-parameters stdlib.module.std::mutable_vector capability.assignment error.mutable-place-receiver
docs: MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md

== WHAT IT IS
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
    v.push(3)                      v now holds [1, 2, 3]
    last = v.pop()                 errors IndexNotFound when empty

After `import std::mutable_vector`: from_list, push, pop, take(i), swap, at,
length, empty?, clear; `loop x in v:` iterates it. Copies share their
elements copy-on-write, so copying is cheap.

== RULES
  * A vector is a value, not a reference: `w = v` and passing `v` to a
    function make logical copies, and mutating one never changes another.
  * The receiver of a mutation must be a place of the function doing it
    (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER): a local binding, a parameter, a context
    parameter, or a field path from one. A call result or a temporary is
    not a place, and a nested function cannot mutate an enclosing
    function's vector (MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE).
  * A callee mutates its own copy: to give a caller the change, return the
    vector, or declare the parameter a place (`place v: MutableVector[T]`,
    concept.place-parameters): then the callee mutates the caller's own.
  * pop, take and at fail with IndexNotFound unless proven safe.
  * A vector of affine elements is affine: it moves whole and elements
    leave it only through pop, take, swap or a consuming loop.
  * `==` is not defined on vectors (a run-time EQUALITY error).

== WHY
Some algorithms need in-place growth; the place rule keeps that mutation
local and visible -- a mutation never rebinds a name or changes a value
some other binding holds -- so value semantics still hold everywhere.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

fn with_extra(v: MutableVector[int]) -> MutableVector[int]:
    v.push(9)
    v

v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
w = with_extra(v)
last = v.pop():
    on IndexNotFound:
        0
[last, v.length(), w.length()]
@caption The callee mutates and returns its own copy
@value [2, 1, 3]
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_vector

fn fresh() -> MutableVector[int]:
    std::mutable_vector::from_list([])

fresh().push(1)
0
@caption A call result is not a place
@error CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER
@at 6:1
@end

