@topic concept.place-parameters
title: Place parameters: `place m: T` updates the caller's collection
summary: A parameter declared `place m: T` is the caller's place itself, so the function mutates the caller's collection (or struct of collections) in place, as the collection's own operations do.
index: concepts
alias: place parameter; place; in-out parameter; inout; mutable parameter; pass by reference; by-reference parameter; out parameter
keywords: place, parameter, mutate, caller, MutableVector, MutableArray, MutableMap, MutableSet, struct, MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER, MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE, overlap
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array concept.mutable-map concept.values concept.functions error.mutable-place-receiver capability.assignment
docs: PLACE-PARAMETERS.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md ORDERED-MUTABLE-MAP-SET.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn push_twice(place v: MutableVector[int], x: int) -> unit:
        v.push(x)
        v.push(x)

    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1])
    push_twice(v, 7)               v now holds [1, 7, 7]

An ordinary parameter is the function's own value (a logical copy of the
argument), so pushing into it changes nothing the caller sees. A place
parameter is the caller's place: the caller passes a place of its own and
every mutation the function makes through the parameter -- a collection
operation on it or on a field path of it, or a call passing it on to
another place parameter -- is the caller's. std::mutable_map's set and
std::mutable_set's add are ordinary functions of this kind.

== RULES
  * The parameter's type is written and holds a mutable collection: a
    MutableVector, a MutableArray, or a struct whose fields hold one (a
    MutableMap is one); it is not affine. Otherwise TYPE.
  * The argument is a place of the caller: a local, a parameter, a context
    parameter's member, or a field path from one -- not a temporary, a
    module binding or an enclosing function's binding
    (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER).
  * One call gives one place to one place parameter only: `f(a, a)` or
    `f(h, h.items)` is MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER.
  * A function with a place parameter is only called, never used as a
    value (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER), and a nested function cannot capture a
    place parameter (MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE). A trait requirement takes
    values: it has no place parameters.
  * Everything else is a value as before: a copy of the place made before
    or after the call, the callee's own copy (`snapshot = v`) and its
    result never observe a later update.
  * `place` marks a parameter only before its name: `fn f(place: int)`
    declares an ordinary parameter named place.

== WHY
Botlish has no references and no assignment: a collection is a value, and
its in-place mutations are made through places (concept.mutable-vector).
A place parameter extends that rule across one call -- the function's
updates are the caller's place's -- without making the collection a
reference: nothing can store, return or capture the place, and nothing of
it exists at run time.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_vector

struct Bag:
    items: MutableVector[int]

fn add(place b: Bag, x: int) -> unit:
    b.items.push(x)

b = Bag {items: std::mutable_vector::from_list([])}
before = b
b.add(1)
b.add(2)
[b.items.length(), before.items.length()]
@caption The caller's bag grows; a copy made before the call does not
@value [2, 0]
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_vector

fn grow(place v: MutableVector[int]) -> unit:
    v.push(1)

grow(std::mutable_vector::from_list([]))
0
@caption A temporary is not a place: its update would be lost
@error CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER
@at 6:6
@end

