@topic concept.collection-order
title: Order, membership and identity of a map or set
summary: A MutableMap's or MutableSet's iteration order is a permutation of its members that reorder may replace, never part of its contents: reorder changes the order only.
index: concepts
alias: reorder; reordering; iteration order; collection order; permutation of keys
keywords: reorder, order, validate_order, OrderMismatch, permutation, members, keys, insertion order, MutableMap, MutableSet
related: concept.mutable-map concept.mutable-set proof.same-members error.reorder-not-proven concept.place-parameters concept.values stdlib.std::mutable_map::reorder stdlib.std::mutable_set::reorder stdlib.std::mutable_map::order stdlib.std::mutable_map::validate_order
docs: PROVEN-COLLECTION-REORDERING.md ORDERED-MUTABLE-MAP-SET.md

== WHAT IT IS
    o = m.order()                  the keys, in m's order: a MutableSet
    o = m.validate_order(c)        C, checked once to hold exactly m's
        on std::mutable_map::OrderMismatch:    keys (any order)
            m.order()
    m.reorder(o)                   m in O's order

A map's *contents* are its associations, a set's its members; its
*order* is a permutation of those members. Insertion order is only the
default history: reorder replaces the order and nothing else -- every
key keeps its stored key and its value, lookups and length are
unchanged, and the next new key still goes last. The same holds for
`s.order()`, `s.validate_order(c)` and `s.reorder(o)` of a set (a copy
of the set is an order of it too).

== RULES
  * The order is a MutableSet (distinct by construction) that must hold
    exactly the collection's current members: the compiler must know it
    (REORDER-NOT-PROVEN), and a proven reorder checks nothing at run time.
  * Changing either value's members (set, remove, clear, add, a place
    call) loses that knowledge; reading either, and reordering either,
    keeps it.
  * A reorder is O(n): it moves the entries with their stored hashes
    (nothing is hashed again) and keeps the table's reservation.
  * Maps and sets are values: a copy reorders independently, and a loop
    iterates the collection as it was when the loop began.

== WHY
Order is under the program's control, not the hidden hash's; and an order
is a permutation of what the collection holds, never an arbitrary
sequence. Equal lengths or matching types prove nothing about which keys
an order holds, so the fact is proven, or checked once, and then trusted.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_map

m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
m.set("pear", 2)
m.set("fig", 1)
o = m.order()
m.reorder(o)
m.set("kiwi", 3)
loop e in m:
    e.key
@caption The current order, then a new key last
@value ["pear", "fig", "kiwi"]
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_set

s = std::mutable_set::create[int]()
s.add(1)
s.add(2)
s.add(3)
want = std::mutable_set::create[int]()
want.add(3)
want.add(1)
want.add(2)
o = s.validate_order(want):
    on std::mutable_set::OrderMismatch:
        s
s.reorder(o)
loop x in s:
    x
@caption A set in a chosen order
@value [3, 1, 2]
@end

