== WHAT IT IS
    m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
    m.set("pear", 3)               a new key goes last
    m.set("pear", 4)               a present key keeps its place
    n = m.at("pear"):              KeyNotFound when absent
        on std::mutable_map::KeyNotFound:
            0
    loop e in m:                   MutableMapEntry {key, value}, in
        e.key                      insertion order

After `import std::mutable_map`: create[K, V](), with_capacity[K, V](n),
length, empty?, contains, at, set, remove (returns the value), clear,
order, validate_order, reorder (concept.collection-order) and
`loop e in m:`. Expected cost: O(1) for contains, at, set and remove,
O(n) for a loop.

== RULES
  * K must satisfy Hashable (TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED otherwise): Ints, Strings,
    enums, and structs and Lists of such values; never a function, a
    collection or `any`. Keys are compared by `==`.
  * Order is insertion order, whatever the keys and on every backend:
    set of a present key replaces only its value; remove then set adds the
    key last. Growth and removal never reorder the rest.
  * at and remove of a missing key fail with the declared error
    std::mutable_map::KeyNotFound (handle it or declare it); there is no
    default value.
  * A map is a value, like a MutableVector: `n = m`, an argument, a result
    or a struct field is a logical copy, and mutating one copy never
    changes another. Mutations take the map as a place
    (concept.place-parameters): `m.set(k, v)` needs `m` to be a place.
  * A loop iterates the map as it was when the loop began: the body may
    mutate the map, which never changes what the loop visits.
  * A map is not Hashable, and `==` of two maps is a run-time EQUALITY
    error: compare contents with a loop and contains/at.

== WHY
The keyed hash that places a key is private and different in every
process (HASHABLE-AND-KEYED-HASH.md); iteration follows insertion order so
that no program can observe it, and a program's output is the same
everywhere.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_map

m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
loop w in ["fig", "pear", "fig", "kiwi", "pear", "fig"]:
    n = m.at(w):
        on std::mutable_map::KeyNotFound:
            0
    m.set(w, n + 1)
loop e in m:
    [e.key, e.value]
@caption Counting words; the order is first occurrence
@value [["fig", 3], ["pear", 2], ["kiwi", 1]]
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_map

m = std::mutable_map::create[int, int]()
m.at(1)
@caption A missing key is a declared error to handle
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 4:1
@end

