== WHAT IT IS
After `import std::mutable_set`: create[T](), with_capacity[T](n), length,
empty?, contains, add (true when it added), remove (true when it removed),
clear, and `loop x in s:`. Expected cost: O(1) for contains, add and
remove, O(n) for a loop.

== RULES
  * T must satisfy Hashable; membership is `==`.
  * Order is insertion order: adding a present element changes nothing,
    removing one and adding it again adds it last.
  * A set is a value (copies are independent), its mutations take it as a
    place, and a loop iterates it as it was when the loop began.
  * A set is not Hashable and has no `==`; std::immutable_set is the
    immutable, comparable set (concept.immutable-set).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_set

s = std::mutable_set::create[int]()
added = loop x in [5, 2, 5, 9, 2]:
    s.add(x)
s.remove(5)
s.add(5)
order = loop x in s:
    x
[added, order]
@caption Duplicates add nothing; removing and re-adding moves an element last
@value [[true, true, false, true, false], [2, 9, 5]]
@end

