@topic concept.mutable-array
title: MutableArray[T]: a fixed-length mutable value
summary: MutableArray[T] has a fixed capacity and indexed reads and writes (at, set, swap, copy, freeze); like MutableVector it is a copy-on-write value mutated only through places.
index: concepts
alias: MutableArray; mutable array; fixed-size array
keywords: mutable_array, create, from_list, generate, at, set, swap, copy, freeze, capacity, place, IndexNotFound, nomethod
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.values concept.lists concept.nomethod proof.list-bounds proof.slices stdlib.module.std::mutable_array error.mutable-place-receiver
docs: MUTABLE-ARRAY.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md PARAMETERIZED-MUTABLEARRAY.md

== WHAT IT IS
    a = std::mutable_array::create(3, 0)          [0, 0, 0]
    a.set(1, 7)                              in place
    xs = a.freeze(3)                         a List copy of slots 0..3

After `import std::mutable_array`: create(n, value), from_list, generate(n,
f), at, set, swap, capacity, copy, freeze, get. It used to have
reference semantics; it is now a value like MutableVector.

== RULES
  * Assignment and argument passing copy (copy-on-write); mutations go
    through places only (MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER, MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE).
  * at and set fail with IndexNotFound, freeze and copy with
    LowerUnderrun/UpperOverrun, unless the compiler proves the index or
    slice valid (proof.list-bounds, proof.slices).
  * create and copy are `nomethod`: their first argument is a length or a
    destination, not a receiver.
  * The capacity never changes; there is no push or pop.
  * `==` is not defined on arrays (a run-time EQUALITY error).

== WHY
A fixed-size table with O(1) writes serves boards, buffers and dynamic
programming without giving up value semantics.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::from_list([1, 2, 3])
a.set(0, 10)
b = a
b.set(1, 20)
[a.freeze(3), b.freeze(3), a.capacity()]
@caption b is a copy: setting it leaves a unchanged
@value [[10, 2, 3], [10, 20, 3], 3]
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_array

a = std::mutable_array::create(3, 0)
a.set(5, 1):
    on IndexNotFound:
        unit
a.capacity()
@caption Index 5 of a capacity-3 array always fails
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 4:1
@end

