@topic concept.immutable-set
title: ImmutableSet[T]: membership tests
summary: ImmutableSet[T] is an immutable set built from a List with std::immutable_set::from_list and queried with contains; it deduplicates by value equality and has no iteration or update operations.
index: concepts
alias: ImmutableSet; hash set; set membership; set type
keywords: immutable_set, set, contains, from_list, membership, deduplicate, std::byte::set, equality
related: concept.lists concept.values stdlib.module.std::immutable_set stdlib.std::immutable_set::contains
docs: MINIMAL-IMMUTABLE-SET.md BYTE-SET.md

== WHAT IT IS
After `import std::immutable_set`: `std::immutable_set::from_list(xs)`
builds an `ImmutableSet[T]` from a `List[T]`, and `s.contains(x)` answers
membership (false for absence; never an error for comparable values).

== RULES
  * Duplicates collapse by `==`; two sets are `==` when they hold the same
    elements, whatever the construction order.
  * There is no insert, remove, size or iteration (`loop x in s:` is a
    run-time TYPE error): keep the List if you need the elements.
  * Membership of a value whose equality is undefined (a function) fails
    at run time with EQUALITY.

== WHY
The set commits only to semantics (membership by value equality), not to
a representation, so the runtime may choose one later; std::byte::set builds
the common `ImmutableSet[Byte]` of characters.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::immutable_set

a = std::immutable_set::from_list([1, 2, 2])
b = std::immutable_set::from_list([2, 1])
[a == b, a.contains(2), a.contains(9)]
@value [true, true, false]
@end

