@topic concept.static-compile
title: `static compile:`: compiler diagnostics as values
summary: A `static compile:` block the compiler accepts runs as written; one it rejects fails with std::compiler::CompilerError, carrying the diagnostic's code and location.
index: concepts
alias: static compile; static compile block; compile-time rejection test; CompilerError
keywords: static, compile, CompilerError, DiagnosticCode, compiler, rejects, diagnostic, test, compile-time, import std::compiler, STATIC-UNKNOWN-OPERATION
related: concept.testing concept.negative-tests concept.errors concept.error-payloads stdlib.module.std::compiler stdlib.std::test::rejects error.static-unknown-operation
docs: STATIC-COMPILE.md STATIC-DECISION-ECONOMICS.md GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md

== WHAT IT IS
    fn probe() -> int errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            missing + 1

The block is decided at compile time by the ordinary compiler with its body
where it stands. Accepted, the boundary disappears: the body runs with its
ordinary value and errors, compiled exactly as if written there. Rejected,
none of it runs and it completes with the payload error
`std::compiler::CompilerError {code, primary, related, message}`: `code` is
a `std::compiler::DiagnosticCode` case (Unbound, Type, KnownError, ...), and
`primary` a `std::compiler::SourceLocation` {line, column}.

== RULES
  * A file using it writes `import std::compiler` (MISSING-IMPORT
    otherwise).
  * A rejected block's CompilerError is handled or admitted like any
    declared error; compare `code`, not the message text.
  * A syntax error inside the block is the program's, not the block's:
    the block captures parsed syntax.
  * It is not an assertion and not compile-time evaluation: the compiler
    never runs the block, it only decides whether it compiles. `compile`
    is the only operation (`static NAME:` with another name is
    STATIC-UNKNOWN-OPERATION).

== WHY
It makes "this code is rejected with this diagnostic" testable from
Botlish itself (concept.testing) without a separate harness, while an
accepted block costs nothing: it is the same code as the body.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::compiler

fn probe() -> int errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
    static compile:
        missing + 1

r = probe():
    on std::compiler::CompilerError {code, primary}:
        if code == std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::Unbound:
            primary.line
        else:
            0
r
@caption The rejected block reports Unbound at line 5
@value 5
@end

@example accept
import std::compiler

fn adds() -> int:
    static compile:
        20 + 22

adds()
@caption An accepted block is its body; no CompilerError is possible
@value 42
@end

