@topic concept.testing
title: Testing with stdlib/test.bot: suites, cases, report
summary: stdlib/test.bot (std::test) is ordinary Botlish: install `std::test::suite()` as a context, register cases with value, holds or rejects, and report() returns PASS/FAIL lines.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: testing; unit test; test framework; golden test; test suite; assert; assertion
keywords: test, suite, case, value, holds, rejects, rejects_at, report, PASS, FAIL, golden, assertion, expect, Match, run
related: concept.negative-tests concept.static-compile concept.contexts concept.coroutines capability.test-timeout capability.process-exit-status stdlib.module.std::test stdlib.std::test::value stdlib.std::test::holds stdlib.std::test::rejects stdlib.std::test::case stdlib.std::test::report stdlib.std::int::to_string
docs: GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md STATIC-COMPILE.md

== WHAT IT IS
    import std::test

    fn register_tests():
        fn adds():
            20 + 22
        std::test::value("adds", 42, adds)

    with context std::test::suite()
    register_tests()
    std::test::report()        ["PASS adds", "1 passed, 0 failed"]

A suite is a context (`std::test::Suite`) holding a MutableVector of cases.
Registration stores a deferred check (a function of no arguments) and never
calls it; std::test::report() runs every case once, in registration order,
through an ordinary coroutine (std::test::run), and returns the report as a
List of Strings: "PASS name", or "FAIL name" with indented
expected/actual/detail lines, then "P passed, F failed".

    std::test::value(name, expected, body)            body() == expected
    std::test::holds(name, description, pred, body)   pred(body()) is true
    std::test::rejects(name, code, body)              body's `static compile:`
                                                 block is rejected with code
    std::test::rejects_at / rejects_message / rejects_containing / rejects_any
    std::test::case(name, check)                      a custom check (a Match)

== RULES
  * There is no assertion statement or test syntax: a failing comparison
    is a value (a Match), so one failing case never stops the run.
  * A body's only allowed declared error is std::compiler::CompilerError;
    any other error it may raise must be handled inside it.
  * Registration must run after `with context std::test::suite()`
    (MISSING-CONTEXT otherwise). The registration helpers are `nomethod`.
  * No per-test timeout: a non-terminating case hangs the suite
    (capability.test-timeout).
  * The report is a value: make it the program's value (as below) or
    print its lines. A FAIL line does not make the program itself fail
    (capability.process-exit-status).
  * A report shows an Int with std::int::to_string's text
    (task.number-text); a test's own texts convert numbers the same way.

== WHY
Tests written in the language they test exercise the real compiler and
every backend; contexts, coroutines and static compile blocks are enough
to build the framework, so it needs no compiler support of its own.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::test

fn double(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

fn even?(n: int) -> bool:
    n.mod(2) == 0

fn register_tests():
    fn doubles():
        double(21)
    std::test::value("double 21", 42, doubles)
    std::test::holds("double is even", "an even number", even?, doubles)

with context std::test::suite()
register_tests()
std::test::report()
@caption Two cases and the report
@value ["PASS double 21", "PASS double is even", "2 passed, 0 failed"]
@end

