for testing failures and rejections.

@topic concept.negative-tests
title: Testing failing calls and compiler rejections
summary: A tested failing call must not be statically known to fail (KNOWN-ERROR): pass the input through a parameter and assert the handled outcome, or test a rejection with static compile.
index: concepts
alias: negative tests; negative test; testing failing calls; test an error; test a failure; test a failing call; expected failure test
keywords: test, failure, error, handler, KNOWN-ERROR, rejects, static compile, parameter, negative, expect error, value, registration
related: concept.testing concept.known-errors concept.static-compile concept.errors rule.known-error error.known-error stdlib.std::test::value stdlib.std::test::rejects
docs: GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md STATIC-COMPILE.md

== WHAT IT IS
How to test a call that fails on purpose. Writing the failing call with a
literal argument inside a test body does not work when the compiler can
prove the failure: a call proven to always fail is KNOWN-ERROR, rejected
even though a handler is present (concept.known-errors). For example
`checked(-1)` with `on Negative:` is rejected when checked's guard
`n < 0` decides the outcome for -1.

== RULES
Two supported patterns:

  * To test the run-time behavior (the error and its handling): route the
    failing input through a parameter of a registration function. Inside,
    the call depends on the parameter, so it is not statically known to
    fail; handle the error and assert the handled outcome with
    std::test::value. Call the registration function with the failing input.
  * To test that the compiler rejects something (including a KNOWN-ERROR
    itself): put the code in a `static compile:` block and register it with
    std::test::rejects(name, std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::CASE, body) (or
    rejects_at to pin the location). The file imports compiler.

Choose a handler value the success path cannot produce (below, -1:
checked never returns a negative number), so the comparison tells the
handled failure apart from a success.

== WHY
KNOWN-ERROR exists because a call that can only fail is dead code wearing
a handler; it protects programs, but it also catches a test that writes
the failure literally. The parameter keeps the test honest -- the same
call a program makes with unknown input -- and the static block states a
compile-time fact as a compile-time test.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::test

error Negative

fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn register_failure(input: int):
    fn run():
        checked(input):
            on Negative:
                -1
    std::test::value("a negative input fails with Negative", -1, run)

with context std::test::suite()
register_failure(-1)
std::test::report()
@caption The failing input is a parameter, so the call is not decided
@value ["PASS a negative input fails with Negative", "1 passed, 0 failed"]
@end

@example accept
import std::compiler
import std::test

error Negative

fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn register_tests():
    fn literal_failure() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            checked(-1):
                on Negative:
                    -1
    std::test::rejects("checked(-1) is a known error",
        std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::KnownError, literal_failure)

with context std::test::suite()
register_tests()
std::test::report()
@caption The rejection itself, tested with static compile and std::test::rejects
@value ["PASS checked(-1) is a known error", "1 passed, 0 failed"]
@end

@example reject
import std::test

error Negative

fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn register_tests():
    fn negative_fails():
        checked(-1):
            on Negative:
                -1
    std::test::value("a negative input fails", -1, negative_fails)

with context std::test::suite()
register_tests()
std::test::report()
@caption A literal failing call in a test body is KNOWN-ERROR
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 12:9
@end

