@topic concept.direct-modules
title: Direct modules: raw authority declared at the module boundary
summary: A module that calls a direct module (linux, a syscall transport) without a context of its own exposes raw authority and must start with `direct module`; the compiler checks the line.
index: concepts
alias: direct module; raw authority; capability safety; ambient authority
keywords: direct, module, authority, capability, syscall, linux, context, DIRECT, STALE-DIRECT-MODULE, DIRECT-CALLABLE-VALUE, mediated
related: concept.contexts concept.modules concept.c-ffi concept.opaque-structs error.direct error.stale-direct-module
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md CONTEXTS.md LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md

== WHAT IT IS
Direct means raw, ambient authority not yet converted into a context: a
syscall, a raw file descriptor, a machine address, a retaining C call. The
syscall transports are direct by declaration, and stdlib/linux.bot is
declared `direct module`. A module stops directness by mediating every such
call through a function that requires a context the module declares itself;
std::linux::io does this with its LinuxIO context, so std::linux::io, io and
everything above them are ordinary.

== RULES
  * A call into another direct module from a function that requires no
    context of the calling module's own makes the module direct: without
    the `direct module` line it is DIRECT; with the line but no such call
    it is STALE-DIRECT-MODULE. The line must state exactly what the
    compiler infers.
  * The entry program is a module like any other: a program that calls
    `linux` or a transport itself starts with `direct module` or mediates
    the calls through a context it declares.
  * A non-direct module may not take a direct callable as a value
    (DIRECT-CALLABLE-VALUE).
  * There is no per-function or per-call form (`unsafe`, `direct fn`):
    the trust unit is the module.

== WHY
A reviewer who wants to know which code can reach raw system authority
reads module headers, not every function; capability-safe modules are
those whose raw calls all go through their own contexts.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
direct module
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::linux
import std::str

fn say(text: str) -> int:
    stdout = std::abi::i32(1)
    stdout.write(std::abi::bytes::from_list(text.encode_utf8()))

say("hi\n")
@caption A raw write(2) of 3 bytes (native backends only)
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@output hi
@value 3
@end

@example reject
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::linux
import std::str

fn say(text: str) -> int:
    stdout = std::abi::i32(1)
    stdout.write(std::abi::bytes::from_list(text.encode_utf8()))

0
@caption The entry program uses direct authority without declaring it
@error CORE SEMANTIC DIRECT
@at 8:5
@end

