directness.

@topic concept.c-ffi
title: Calling C: `extern c fn` and `extern c library`
summary: `extern c fn` declares a C function by its ABI signature and pointer contracts, `extern c library` a library to link; C calls run only on the native backends.
index: concepts
alias: C FFI; extern c; foreign function interface; call C function
keywords: extern, c, ffi, foreign, library, link, std::ffi::c, Ptr, Int, noescape, borrow, NATIVE-ONLY, bindgen
related: concept.direct-modules concept.binary-layouts concept.c-bindgen-layouts concept.c-floats concept.native-link-plan backend.native-only backend.native-executable error.native-only
docs: C-FFI.md C-ABI-TYPES.md C-POINTER-BORROWING.md C-BINDGEN.md C-FLOAT-FFI-MILESTONE-1.md NATIVE-LINK-PLAN-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
    import std::ffi::c
    extern c library "crypto"
    extern c fn abs(n: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int

A truthful C declaration with no body. Types come from `std::ffi::c`: the C
integer scalars (`std::ffi::c::Int`, `std::ffi::c::ULong`, ... -- integer
domains whose ranges are the compilation target's), C's float and double
(`std::ffi::c::Float`, `std::ffi::c::Double`: Float64 values,
concept.c-floats), `std::ffi::c::Void`, pointers `std::ffi::c::Ptr[T]` and
`std::ffi::c::FnPtr[...]`. A pointer parameter may borrow existing Bytes or
MutableBytes storage for the one call when its contract says `read noescape`
or `write noescape`.

== RULES
  * The Tcl backends refuse a C call at run time (NATIVE-ONLY): run with
    `-backend cranelift` or build a native executable. Symbols are resolved
    by the system linker at link time, never at run time.
  * No fact comes from a name or a C type: an absent escape or access fact
    is unknown, which never admits a byte exposure.
  * Pointers are affine C ABI values: no arithmetic, dereference or integer
    conversion. The memory a data pointer addresses is read only through a
    layout's native view (std::binary::native::view, concept.binary-layouts)
    and written only through its native edit (std::binary::native::edit,
    std::binary::store). A record C expects a pointer to is built in byte
    storage (std::binary::write; std::binary::write_foreign for its pointer
    fields) and passed by the borrowing contract.
  * A retaining call (a pointer that may escape) is direct authority
    (concept.direct-modules).
  * A variadic C function ends its parameters with the bare word
    `variadic` (C's `...`); each call's trailing arguments are C ABI
    values, promoted as C promotes them (concept.variadics).
  * tools/c-bindgen.tcl generates such declarations from real headers,
    and a layout for every C record a layout represents, as the target's C
    compiler lays it out (concept.c-bindgen-layouts).
  * `extern c library "NAME" static` (optionally `at "PATH"`) requires a
    static archive, `extern c framework "NAME"` links an Apple framework
    (concept.native-link-plan).

== WHY
C interop reuses the platform ABI and linker for the machine problem and
Botlish's existing value, borrowing and capability rules for the language
problem, instead of building a second C ecosystem inside the compiler.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn abs(n: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int

abs(-5)
@caption libc's abs, linked and called natively
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@value 5
@end

