@topic concept.native-link-plan
title: Native dependencies: static libraries, archive paths and Apple frameworks
summary: `extern c library "NAME" static` requires a static archive, `static at "PATH"` names its file from the application root, and `extern c framework "NAME"` links an Apple framework.
index: concepts backends
alias: static linking; link a static archive; static archive path; extern c framework; Apple framework; -framework; link plan; native dependencies
keywords: extern c library, static, at, archive, .a, .lib, import library, framework, -framework, -F, -L, link plan, application root, LINK-CONFLICT, NATIVE LINK ARCHIVE, BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINK_LOG
related: concept.c-ffi backend.native-executable backend.native-library error.native-link error.link-conflict error.unsupported-platform cli.options
docs: NATIVE-LINK-PLAN-MILESTONE-1.md C-FFI.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE-FFI-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
    extern c library "m"                                     the linker's choice
    extern c library "SDL3" static                           libSDL3.a, never libSDL3.so
    extern c library "SDL3" static at "native/libSDL3.a"     exactly that file
    extern c framework "Cocoa"                               -framework Cocoa (macOS)

Every declaration of a program, its imported modules' included, is one entry
of its link plan, in first-declaration order. A library without `static` is
the platform linker's to find, shared or static (-lNAME; NAME.lib on
Windows), as it always was. `static` is a requirement: the executable
incorporates the library's static archive and never depends on a shared
library of that name. `at PATH` names the archive: a path relative to the
application root (the entry file's directory, whatever the working
directory and whichever application module declares it) -- for a module of
an installed package (.bot_modules), the package's own directory -- unless
it is absolute, written with `/` between its components on every host. A
framework is Apple's: Apple's ld links it from the system's or a -F
directory.

== RULES
  * Names and paths are string literals: a name is letters, digits,
    `_ + . -`, never starting with `-` (never a path or a linker option);
    a path is a file's, never empty, a directory or holding a `\`.
  * Declarations of one library merge into the strictest: `"SDL3"` and
    `"SDL3" static at "native/libSDL3.a"` (a generated binding's and the
    application's) are the archive. Two different archive paths, or a
    library and a framework of one name, are LINK-CONFLICT
    (error.link-conflict): neither is chosen.
  * A framework is refused for a target whose linker has none
    (UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM: every Linux and Windows target), and makes the
    program's HIR the macOS target's.
  * Checking needs no file: the front end validates the declarations, the
    link checks the files. Before the platform linker runs the driver finds
    each static archive (the -L directories, then the toolchain's) and
    checks it is a regular file holding objects of the target's format and
    architecture -- never a shared library, a `.tbd` stub, a linker script
    or a Windows import library (a `.lib` that imports from a DLL) -- and
    fails with NATIVE LINK ARCHIVE otherwise (error.native-link). The
    archive is passed to the linker by its absolute path, read again at
    every link: rebuilding it changes the next executable.
  * The static library of -emit-native-library holds none of a program's
    native dependencies: its manifest lists them (`dependencies`) for the
    consumer's link; its shared library is linked with them.

== WHY
Which implementation of a foreign interface becomes part of an application,
and whether it must be incorporated, is part of the program's contract: a
deployed executable that silently picked up a shared library would need it
installed wherever it runs. The platform linker still links; Botlish states
the intent, checks the inputs it can check, and gives the linker the file.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
extern c library "z" static

1 + 1
@caption Checking needs no archive: the link plan is checked when the program is linked
@backends interp compile
@value 2
@end

@example accept
extern c framework "CoreFoundation"

1 + 1
@caption A framework, for a macOS target (the Tcl backends call no C)
@target macos-aarch64
@backends interp compile
@value 2
@end

@example reject
import binding

1
@module binding
extern c library "SDL3" static at "native/libSDL3.a"
extern c library "SDL3" static at "vendor/libSDL3.a"
@caption Two archives of one library
@error CORE SEMANTIC LINK-CONFLICT
@at binding.bot:2:35
@end

