summary: C's float (binary32) and double (binary64) are C representations of Float64 values, rounded to a float on the way out and never NaN or an infinity on the way in.
index: concepts
alias: C float; C double; std::ffi::c::Float; std::ffi::c::Double; float32; binary32; single precision
keywords: float, double, std::ffi::c::Float, std::ffi::c::Double, binary32, binary64, NonFiniteFloat, round to nearest, ties to even, subnormal, negative zero, long double, layout, extern
related: concept.floats concept.c-ffi concept.binary-layouts concept.c-bindgen-layouts error.known-error
docs: C-FLOAT-FFI-MILESTONE-1.md C-FFI.md C-ABI-TYPES.md FLOAT64-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
    import std::ffi::c
    extern c fn scale(x: std::ffi::c::Float, k: std::ffi::c::Double) -> std::ffi::c::Float

    layout Sample:
        size 16
        align 8
        field f: std::ffi::c::Float at 0
        field d: std::ffi::c::Double at 8

std::ffi::c::Float is C's float and std::ffi::c::Double C's double, the IEEE
754 binary32 and binary64 on every target. They are written only where a C
type is: an extern c fn's parameters and result, a function pointer's
signature, a pointee (std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Float]) and a layout
field or array element. Their values are Float64s: a float crosses as a
Float64, and Botlish has no binary32 number of its own.

== RULES
  * Leaving Botlish for a float, a Float64 is rounded to nearest, ties to
    even (a subnormal float stays one; a negative number too small for a
    float is +0). One whose rounding is an infinity -- from 2^128 - 2^103
    up -- fails with NonFiniteFloat of its sign before C runs (or before a
    byte is written), never clamped to the largest float. A double
    receives the Float64 exactly.
  * Entering Botlish, a float or a double that is NaN (any payload) or an
    infinity fails with NonFiniteFloat {result: NotANumber,
    PositiveInfinity or NegativeInfinity}; negative zero becomes 0.0; every
    finite float widens exactly.
  * A call declares NonFiniteFloat only where it can fail through one: an
    extern with a float parameter or a floating result, a layout read of a
    float or a double, a write or emit of a float (never of a double, and
    never an operation of a layout without floats). When the compiler
    knows a float argument exactly it applies the same rule while
    compiling: an overflow is KNOWN-ERROR, one that fits needs no handler.
  * A field read through a view (v.f) cannot carry its own handler: read
    a float field in a function that declares NonFiniteFloat, or copy the
    record with std::binary::load, a call a handler may follow.
  * std::ffi::c::Float and std::ffi::c::Double are distinct types, never
    Float64: Ptr[std::ffi::c::Float] is not Ptr[std::ffi::c::Double], an
    array of floats strides 4 bytes, and a value type (a parameter of a
    Botlish function) is written Float64 (std::ffi::c::Double means Float64
    there; std::ffi::c::Float is a TYPE error). Nothing converts implicitly,
    and a DecimalRounded value is never a C float or double (decay it with
    std::float64::from_decimal_rounded).
  * C long double and the other floating types (_Float16, __float128) have
    no FFI type: tools/c-bindgen.tcl reports them unsupported. Botlish has
    no indirect call, so a C callback of a float signature is a function
    pointer C calls, never Botlish.

== WHY
A binary32 is a representation, not a number Botlish computes with: the
one Float64 type keeps arithmetic, equality and proofs as they are, and
the rounding and the finiteness rules apply exactly where C's bits cross,
the same at compile time and at run time on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::ffi::c

layout Sample:
    size 16
    align 8
    field f: std::ffi::c::Float at 0
    field d: std::ffi::c::Double at 8

fn round_trip(x: Float64) -> list errors LayoutLengthMismatch, NonFiniteFloat:
    m = std::binary::write(std::binary::write(std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(16)), Sample.f, x), Sample.d, x)
    s = std::binary::parse(Sample, std::abi::bytes::freeze(m))
    [s.f, s.d]

r = round_trip(0.1):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        []
    on NonFiniteFloat:
        []
r
@caption 0.1 rounded to the nearest float, and kept exactly by a double
@value [0.10000000149011612, 0.1]
@end

@example reject
import std::ffi::c

fn half(x: std::ffi::c::Float) -> Float64:
    x / 2.0

half(1.0)
@caption A Botlish parameter holds a Float64: no value is a binary32
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 3:8
@end

@example reject
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn takes(x: std::ffi::c::Float) -> std::ffi::c::Int

takes(1.0e39)
@caption A known Float64 too large for a float: the call can never complete
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 5:1
@end

