@topic concept.c-bindgen-layouts
title: Generated C record layouts: a target's and a toolchain's facts
summary: c-bindgen writes a `layout` for each C record a layout represents, with the target C compiler's size, alignment and offsets: a fact of that target and toolchain only.
index: concepts
alias: generated layout; C struct layout; c-bindgen layouts; layouts.bot; Win32 layouts; MinGW layouts
keywords: bindgen, layout, struct, C record, sizeof, alignof, offsetof, padding, packed, target, toolchain, windows-x86_64-gnu, mingw, Win32
related: concept.c-ffi concept.binary-layouts backend.targets error.layout-field-bounds
docs: C-BINDGEN-LAYOUTS.md C-BINDGEN.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md

== WHAT IT IS
A generated binding (generated/c/PROFILE/TARGET/) has a module NS::layouts
beside NS::ctypes: one ordinary `layout` per complete C record whose every
field is a C integer scalar, a data or function pointer or a nested
generated layout, e.g. Win32's

    # struct _FILETIME, typedef FILETIME (minwindef.h:222): sizeof 8, _Alignof 4
    layout FILETIME:
        size 8
        align 4
        field dwLowDateTime: std::ffi::c::ULong at 0   # DWORD dwLowDateTime
        field dwHighDateTime: std::ffi::c::ULong at 4  # DWORD dwHighDateTime

Every number is the C compiler's answer for the binding's target (sizeof,
_Alignof, __builtin_offsetof, a member's type), never a rule the generator
or the compiler applies; a generated C file of _Static_asserts
(layouts-check.c) checks them against the compiler again. The layout reads
and writes C memory through the ordinary operations
(std::binary::native::view, std::binary::native::edit, std::binary::write);
the record's pointee identity stays NS::ctypes' opaque type, and a pointer
field keeps its pointee's identity.

== RULES
  * A generated layout is its target's: the widths of std::ffi::c's scalars
    are the compilation's target's, so a layout generated for Windows
    (LLP64: a 32-bit long) does not describe, or even compile as, a Linux
    one. Compile a binding for the target it was generated for (-target).
  * It is also its toolchain's. The Win32 binding is generated with
    MinGW-w64's headers and compiler for windows-x86_64-gnu (Windows x86-64,
    the GNU ABI), never for MSVC's windows-x86_64: MSVC's headers and
    layouts are not probed, and a GNU layout is never taken for MSVC's.
  * A fixed array is a layout field and a C union a union layout; an
    anonymous union, or an anonymous struct inside a union, is a layout of
    its own (OVERLAPPED_member3) held by a generated field, its size and
    alignment clang's record layout. A record with a field no layout
    represents yet (a flexible array, a bitfield, float or double, C
    _Bool) gets no layout, and neither does a record or union holding one
    by value; a pointer to it is still an ordinary typed pointer.
    layouts.tsv and the report name each record's blockers, and map every
    member name an anonymous member promotes to its generated path.
  * Nothing in the compiler knows a layout was generated: it is ordinary
    source, checked like any other.

== WHY
The C compiler decides a record's layout; the binding records the result.
Botlish implements no C layout rule (packing, alignment attributes, the
Windows bitfield and long double rules), so a layout can only be as right
as the compiler that answered, and only for the target it answered for.

== EXAMPLES
@example reject
import std::binary
import std::ffi::c

layout FILETIME:
    size 8
    align 4
    field dwLowDateTime: std::ffi::c::ULong at 0
    field dwHighDateTime: std::ffi::c::ULong at 4

std::binary::layout_length(FILETIME)
@caption Win32's FILETIME, generated for windows-x86_64-gnu, compiled for linux-x86_64 (a 64-bit unsigned long)
@target linux-x86_64
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS
@at 8:49
@end

== MORE
C-BINDGEN-LAYOUTS.md; C-BINDGEN.md; `tclsh9.0 tools/c-bindgen.tcl report
PROFILE -target TARGET -why NAME` explains one record's layout or blockers.

@topic concept.binary-layouts
title: Binary layouts: `layout`, std::binary::parse and std::binary::emit
summary: A `layout` declares a fixed-size byte format with explicit size, alignment and field offsets; std::binary::parse imprints bytes into an immutable LayoutStruct[L] and std::binary::emit writes it back.
index: concepts
alias: binary layout; layout declaration; parse bytes; byte format
keywords: layout, binary, parse, emit, LayoutStruct, size, align, field, offset, bytes, LayoutLengthMismatch, little-endian, U32, LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS, array, fixed array, LayoutExtentMismatch, union, union layout
related: concept.structs concept.integer-domains concept.c-ffi stdlib.module.std::binary error.layout-field-bounds concept.layout-unions
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md ABI-BYTES.md NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md LAYOUT-UNIONS.md

== WHAT IT IS
    layout Header:
        size 8
        align 4
        field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0
        field count: std::binary::I16 at 4

`std::binary::parse(Header, data)` turns a std::abi::bytes::Bytes of exactly
8 bytes into a `LayoutStruct[Header]`, an ordinary immutable struct with
fields magic and count; `std::binary::emit(h)` produces the 8 bytes (padding
zero); `std::binary::layout_length(Header)` is the constant 8. A value can
also be built directly: `LayoutStruct[Header] {magic: 7, count: -2}`.

== RULES
  * Everything is explicit: size, a power-of-two alignment and every
    field's offset; padding is the bytes no field covers. Fields must fit
    inside the size (LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS) and may not overlap.
  * A scalar field's type is an integer domain whose values are exactly one
    fixed-width encoding's (std::binary::U8 .. U64, I8 .. I64, or your own
    `Int in 0..65535`); nested layouts are allowed by value. Byte order is
    the target's (little-endian).
  * std::binary::parse fails with LayoutLengthMismatch unless the input has
    exactly the layout's size. A field may be a C float or double
    (std::ffi::c::Float, 4 bytes; std::ffi::c::Double, 8): its bytes holding
    a NaN or an infinity fail a read with NonFiniteFloat, and a Float64 too
    large for a float fails a write or an emit (concept.c-floats); nothing
    else makes emit fail.
  * A layout is not a value or a type: it is named only as the static
    operand of the binary operations, in LayoutStruct[L], NativeView[L]
    and NativeEdit[L], and as the root of a field designator (L.field).
  * The same layout reads live native memory: std::binary::native::view(L,
    p) is a read-only NativeView[L] whose fields are read when projected,
    and std::binary::load(v) its LayoutStruct[L]. A field may be a C
    pointer; such a layout is native-only (LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY): bytes never
    become a pointer (NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md).
  * Writes name one field statically (L.field, L.inner.x):
    std::binary::write(data, L.f, v) is a new MutableBytes with the field
    written; std::binary::write_foreign encodes an existing pointer into a
    pointer field; std::binary::native::edit(L, p) is a NativeEdit[L] --
    live native memory read like a view and written by std::binary::store --
    write authority, never ownership (NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md).
  * A field may be a fixed array, `field name: array 16 of std::ffi::c::Char
    at 8`, of any element representation (rows: `array 2 of array 3 of
    std::binary::U16`; records: `array 4 of Point`), element i at the offset
    plus i times the element's width. Its copied value is a List of exactly
    that many elements (LayoutExtentMismatch otherwise, when emitted or
    written); an array of characters is integers, never a String. Through a
    view it is a live array, NativeView[array N of E]:
    std::binary::element(a, i), std::binary::store_element(e.items, i, v)
    and std::binary::load(a); a designator names an element statically
    (L.items[3]) (FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md).
  * Fields never overlap. Overlap is declared only by a union layout,
    `union layout U:`, several interpretations of the same bytes (every
    alternative at its first byte) held by value in one field; it has no
    copied value, so its alternatives are read and written one at a time
    (concept.layout-unions, LAYOUT-UNIONS.md).

== WHY
A layout owns representation and ordinary structs own meaning: reading
and writing are two directions of one declaration, so a format cannot be
parsed one way and written another.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0
    field count: std::binary::I16 at 4

h = LayoutStruct[Header] {magic: 7, count: -2}
data = std::binary::emit(h)
back = std::binary::parse(Header, data):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        h
[back.magic, back.count, back == h, std::binary::layout_length(Header)]
@caption Emit and parse round-trip
@value [7, -2, true, 8]
@end

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 6

std::binary::layout_length(Header)
@caption A 4-byte field at offset 6 does not fit in 8 bytes
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS
@at 6:38
@end

