@topic concept.layout-unions
title: Union layouts: several interpretations of the same bytes
summary: `union layout U:` declares alternatives that all start at its first byte: explicit overlap with no tag, active alternative or copied value, so each alternative is read and written alone.
index: concepts
alias: union; union layout; overlay; overlapping fields; alternative; C union; anonymous union
keywords: union, alternative, overlap, overlay, interpretation, punning, NativeView, NativeEdit, std::binary::read, LAYOUT-UNION, OVERLAPPED, LARGE_INTEGER, tagged union, sum type, active member
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::read error.layout-union concept.c-bindgen-layouts concept.enums concept.c-ffi
docs: LAYOUT-UNIONS.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md

== WHAT IT IS
    union layout WORD:
        size 4
        align 4
        alternative whole: std::binary::U32
        alternative halves: HALVES
        alternative bytes: array 4 of std::binary::U8

Arrays repeat representation; unions overlap it. Each alternative is a
representation of the union's bytes from its first byte: a scalar, a C
pointer, a nested layout, a fixed array or another union. Choosing one is
interpretation, never conversion: `std::binary::read(data, WORD.halves)`
reads the four bytes as two U16s, `v.whole` through a native view the same
bytes as a U32.

== RULES
  * Overlap must be declared. An ordinary layout's fields still never
    share a byte (LAYOUT-FIELD-OVERLAP); a union's alternatives always do,
    every one at offset 0 (an alternative states no offset).
  * The size and alignment are explicit, never computed from the
    alternatives: each alternative must fit (LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS), and the
    union may be larger (tail padding). Held by value, its width -- and an
    array of unions' stride -- is its declared size.
  * No active alternative and no tag: writing one alternative and reading
    another reads the other's encoding of the same bytes, and nothing
    records what was written. A union is not a sum type (concept.enums):
    no case, discriminant, exhaustiveness or pattern match.
  * No copied value: there is no LayoutStruct of a union or of anything
    holding one, so std::binary::parse, std::binary::emit, std::binary::load
    and whole- array writes of one are LAYOUT-UNION. Each alternative is
    read and written alone, through a designator (L.u.alt.field) or a native
    view or edit.
  * Through a view or an edit a union is the live union, NativeView[U] /
    NativeEdit[U]: an alternative read is one load at its address, a store
    writes exactly the alternative's bytes (the rest of the union keeps
    its bytes), and nothing is cached between reads.
  * Pointer authority is recursive: bytes never become a pointer through
    any alternative (std::binary::read of a pointer alternative is
    LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY). Native memory is raw: a holder of write authority
    may store an integer alternative and read the pointer alternative of
    the same bytes, as C does -- which is why making an edit is direct.
  * A generated C binding writes C unions and anonymous overlays as union
    layouts with the C compiler's size, alignment and offsets
    (concept.c-bindgen-layouts).

== WHY
A C union, an OVERLAPPED's anonymous union, a LARGE_INTEGER describe the
same bytes several ways. Recording them truthfully needs explicit overlap,
and nothing more: which interpretation a protocol means at a given moment
is the protocol's (a tag field beside it, a call's contract), so a wrapper
that knows the protocol reads the matching alternative.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout HALVES:
    size 4
    align 2
    field low: std::binary::U16 at 0
    field high: std::binary::I16 at 2

union layout WORD:
    size 4
    align 4
    alternative whole: std::binary::U32
    alternative halves: HALVES

fn f(d: std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes) -> list errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    e = std::abi::bytes::freeze(std::binary::write(d, WORD.halves.high, -1))
    [std::binary::read(e, WORD.whole), std::binary::read(e, WORD.halves)]

r = f(std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(4))):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        []
r
@caption Written through one alternative, read through two
@value [4294901760, LayoutStruct[HALVES] {low: 0, high: -1}]
@end

@example reject
import std::binary

union layout WORD:
    size 4
    align 4
    alternative whole: std::binary::U32

fn f(w: LayoutStruct[WORD]) -> int:
    0

0
@caption A union has no copied value
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-UNION
@end

