@topic concept.specialization
title: Generic functions, semantic instances and specialization
summary: An untyped parameter makes a function generic; the compiler re-analyzes its body for each call's argument types (a semantic instance) and may compile specialized copies of it.
index: concepts
alias: generic function; generics; semantic instance; call-site specialization; type parameters
keywords: generic, untyped, instance, specialization, monomorphization, any, -aot-spec, cranelift-generic, type variable, template
related: concept.types concept.functions concept.traits backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.performance error.type
docs: README.md OPPORTUNISTIC-SEMANTIC-INSTANCES.md STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md

== WHAT IT IS
Botlish has no type parameters (`fn f[T]` does not exist). A function
with untyped parameters accepts any argument; where a call's argument
types are known, the compiler analyzes the function's ordinary body once
more under those types:

  * a semantic instance (analysis): `first_or([1, 2], 0)` has the result
    type int, so the result can satisfy a typed parameter;
  * a specialized instance (code): the native backend compiles a copy for
    those argument kinds, removing run-time kind checks.

== RULES
  * Instances never change behavior: a function value is always the
    generic function, and the cranelift-generic backend (no
    specialization) gives the same values and errors.
  * An instance never rescues a definition: an obligation of the
    function's own body (a declared result it does not prove) is reported
    at the definition.
  * Typed parameters are contracts checked at the call; untyped ones are
    checked through the instance's analysis, so a wrong argument type is
    still a compile-time TYPE error where the instance proves it.
  * Bounded: at most 8 specialized instances per function.
  * `botlish -aot-spec FILE` shows the instances and their types.

== WHY
Untyped helpers stay reusable across element types without generics
syntax, and calls through them stay precisely typed and fast; the
semantics stay those of the generic function.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list
import std::str

fn first_or(xs, fallback):
    xs.get(0, fallback)

fn shout(s: str) -> str:
    s.concat("!!")

a = [1, 2].first_or(0) + 1
b = ["ab", "cd"].first_or("none").shout()
[a, b]
@caption One generic function, typed precisely at each call
@value [2, "ab!!"]
@end

@example reject
import std::list

fn first_or(xs, fallback):
    xs.get(0, fallback)

fn shout(s: str) -> str:
    s

shout([1, 2].first_or(0))
@caption The instance proves an int, which is not a str
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 9:7
@end

