docs/README.md section 21; OPPORTUNISTIC-SEMANTIC-INSTANCES.md.

@topic concept.program-value
title: A program's value: its last statement
summary: Running a program evaluates the entry file's statements in order; the value of the last one is the program's value, which the compiler prints after `value:` (and an executable prints).
index: concepts
alias: program result; program value; last expression value
keywords: value, result, last expression, print, main, output, entry program, top level, unit
related: concept.unit concept.argv task.print-output cli.output cli.exit-status capability.process-exit-status
docs: README.md

== WHAT IT IS
An entry program is a sequence of top-level statements; module values are
initialized first, then the statements run in order. The last statement's
value is the program's: an expression, a binding (its bound value), an
`if` (its branch's value), a declaration of a function (the function), or
unit for an empty program. `botlish FILE.bot` prints it as

    == FILE.bot (interp)
       value: 42

with Strings quoted and Lists, structs and enum cases shown as values.

== RULES
  * There is no `main` function: the top level is the program.
  * A program that wants to report several things returns a List or a
    struct of them as its value; printed text goes through io (native
    backends; task.print-output).
  * An error that escapes the top level is a compile-time UNHANDLED-ERROR
    for declared errors; run-time failures (ARITHMETIC, a native's TYPE
    check) stop the program with the error.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
total = 6 * 7
{answer: total, label: "the answer", parts: [6, 7]}
@caption The last expression is the program's value
@value {answer: 42, label: "the answer", parts: [6, 7]}
@end

@example accept
x = 5
@caption A final binding: the bound value
@value 5
@end

