@topic concept.argv
title: argv(): the process arguments
summary: argv() returns the process argument vector as a List of Strings, including argument zero, and fails with the declared error InvalidArgumentEncoding when an argument is not valid UTF-8.
index: concepts
alias: command-line arguments; process arguments; argument vector
keywords: argv, arguments, command line, InvalidArgumentEncoding, UTF-8, -argv, input, parameters
related: concept.errors concept.program-value task.read-arguments cli.options capability.input stdlib.argv
docs: ARGV.md

== WHAT IT IS
`argv()` is a root native (no import): `argv() -> List[str] errors
InvalidArgumentEncoding`. Element zero is the invocation name the
launcher supplied, not necessarily a path. Repeated calls observe the same
arguments.

== RULES
  * Every call must handle (or admit) InvalidArgumentEncoding.
  * the compiler (botlish FILE) gives a program the one-element default
    ["botlish-runner"]; `-argv TEXT` (repeatable, argument zero first)
    replaces it, `-argv-hex HEX` passes raw bytes and `-argv-none` the empty
    vector. A standalone executable reads its real process arguments.
  * Arguments are Strings: there is no Int parsing in the standard
    library.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

args = argv():
    on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
        []
std::list::length(args)
@caption Argument zero only, by default
@value 1
@end

@example reject
argv()
@caption The encoding error must be handled
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 1:1
@end

== MORE
ARGV.md; task.read-arguments.
